std:futures.documentation:
An explicitly started asynchronous operation. await job consumes it and returns T, or throws AsyncError<E> after cleanup. Cannot be constructed directly, cloned or awaited twice. cancel requests cancellation; awaiting still accounts for completion.

```tsrust
const job = calculate().start();
const value = await job;
```

Ownership: must be awaited or transferred to an owner that settles it on every reachable exit.

Async operation failure. code is worker, cancelled, panic, timeout, range, empty or closed. cause contains the original typed thrown value for worker failures, otherwise undefined. No constructor or mutation is required.

```tsrust
try {
  await job;
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.code);
}
```

Ownership: ordinary owned error data.

Owned sending endpoint from Channel.sender(). send waits for queue capacity; a closed channel throws SendError<T> code closed retaining the unsent value. Dropping an endpoint releases it.

```tsrust
const sender = queue.sender();
await sender.send(42);
```

Ownership: may move into an async function; cannot be cloned implicitly.

The same SendError<T> identity exported by std:threads. code is full or closed for trySend, and closed for awaited send. value retains the exact unsent owned message; no implicit clone occurs. Cancellation instead disposes a pending message.

```tsrust
try {
  await sender.send(9);
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.value);
}
```

Ownership: explicit clone independently copies the error only when its payload is cloneable; resources and callable environments remain non-cloneable.

TaskMethods.documentation:
Schedules this task on the async runtime and immediately returns its unique job handle. Takes no arguments. The task may execute before start returns. Inputs and completion must own transferable data; caller borrows cannot cross scheduling. Call inside an async function.

```tsrust
const job = calculate().start();
const result = await job;
```

Ownership: consumes this task. The returned job must be settled; no implicit detach.

Job.documentation:
Requests cancellation of this job. Returns true only for the first request that wins before completion; repeated requests or completed work return false. Cancellation is observed at a scheduling point, so synchronous blocking code is not forcibly interrupted. No arguments or defaults.

```tsrust
job.cancel();

try {
  await job;
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.code);
}
```

Ownership: borrows the handle. A request alone does not settle it; await still waits for child cleanup.

AsyncSender.documentation:
Creates another independently closable send endpoint for the same channel. Takes no arguments and returns immediately without copying messages or creating a queue. Closing this sender leaves the new endpoint usable until the receiver closes. Sharing an already closed sender returns another closed endpoint; it does not reopen anything.

```tsrust
import { Channel } from 'std:futures';
async function main(): Task<void> {
  const queue = new Channel<int>(1);
  const first = queue.sender();
  const second = first.share();
  first.close();
  unwrap(second.trySend(42));
  console.log(await queue.receive());
}
```
Prints 42. Ownership: borrows this endpoint and returns an owned endpoint retaining the same queue.

std:result.documentation:
Compatibility import of the global unwrap. Call unwrap without an import in new code; this export keeps existing `import { unwrap } from 'std:result'` code working with identical behavior.

Returns the Ok payload or throws the original Err payload through ordinary try/catch/finally. Takes one Result, with no defaults or additional options. Result<void, E> returns no value on success. Errors retain their type and owned contents; unwrap does not panic or stringify them. Use match when each failure needs local recovery.

Ownership: consumes the Result and moves its active payload without cloning. Calls must be direct; polymorphic function values are unsupported.

```tsrust
import { unwrap } from 'std:result';
import { spawnScoped, join } from 'std:threads';

function main() {
  const first = unwrap(spawnScoped((): int => 20));
  const second = unwrap(spawnScoped((): int => 22));
  const a = unwrap(join(first));
  const b = unwrap(join(second));
  console.log(a + b);
}
```

Expected output: 42.

std:threads.documentation:
Creates a bounded first-in, first-out queue with one receiver. `capacity` (1 to 1000000, required) is the number of unread messages it can hold, not bytes or workers; an invalid value throws `RangeError`.

With capacity 2, two sends finish before any receive and a third send waits until one is received; trySend returns full at once instead, keeping the value. This backpressure stops producers from building an unbounded queue. Create senders with `receiver.sender()` before receiving: receiving with no senders closes the channel. Messages cannot contain channel endpoints.

Ownership: the receiver owns unread messages; dropping it closes the channel.

```tsrust
import { Channel, spawn } from 'std:threads';

function listen(inbox: Channel<string>): void {
  while (true) {
    match (inbox.receive()) {
      Ok(message) => console.log(message),
      Err(_) => {
        return;
      },
    }
  }
}

function main() {
  const inbox = new Channel<string>(8);
  const messages = inbox.sender();
  const worker = unwrap(spawn((): void => listen(inbox)));
  unwrap(messages.send('hello'));
  unwrap(messages.send('goodbye'));
  messages.close();
  unwrap(worker.join());
}
// hello
// goodbye

