console.log(values.some((x: int): boolean => {
  return x > 2;
})); // prints "true"
```

The callback receives each element, its index and the array; the callback may declare fewer parameters. Elements are lent to it read-only. For an array of functions it can call each one, which takes exclusive receiver access. An error thrown in the callback stops the loop and propagates. Extra optional parameters receive `undefined`; a rest parameter receives a fresh owning array, empty after these three arguments.

```tsrust
const values = [3, 1, 2];
console.log(values.some((value: int, index: int, source: ReadOnly<int[]>, extra: int = 2): boolean => {
  return value > extra;
}));
// true
```

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [2, 3];
console.log(values.some((value: int, index: int, source: int[], ...tail: int[]): boolean => {
  return value > 2 && tail.length === 0;
}));
// true
```

Ownership: borrows the array for the loop; nothing is copied.

