Requires mutable access to the array. The removed string, array or record moves into the result without cloning. Sparse arrays are not supported. `users.pop()!` asserts an element is present (an empty array fails at run time); `?? fallback` replaces only `undefined`, `|| fallback` any falsy value. When the array holds functions, a removal statement drops the removed function there, freeing what it captured; a local result owns only the removed element's captures, and leaving its lexical scope releases those captures.

```tsrust
type Holder = {
  run: ReadOnly<Function<int>>;
};

function main(): void {
  const first = [3];
  const second = [5];
  const handlers: Holder[] = [{
    run: (): int => first[0]
  }, {
    run: (): int => second[0]
  }];
  handlers.pop();
  second[0] = 9;
  console.log(handlers[0].run(), second[0]);
}
// 3 9
```

`void[]` has no `pop`; use `length` or `splice`.

Ownership: the removed element moves to the caller; the rest stay in the array.

Removes and returns the first element; the rest move down one index. An empty array returns undefined and stays empty; otherwise length decreases by one. Takes no arguments (any argument is an arity error).

```tsrust
