Returns the element at `index` without changing the array, or `undefined` for an out-of-range index. A negative index counts from the end, so `-1` is the last element.

Takes exactly one int (no conversion from `number`). A stored `null` is returned as `null`; declared union elements are supported.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [10, 20, 30];
console.log(String(values.at(0)), String(values.at(-1)), values.length); // 10 30 3
const empty: int[] = [];
console.log(String(empty.at(0)), String(empty.at(-1)));
const single: int[] = [7];
console.log(String(single.at(1)), String(single.at(-2)));
// undefined undefined
// undefined undefined
```

On a named array of strings, arrays or records, the result is a scoped read-only view: the array cannot change while it is used, and `clone(view)` makes an owned copy. On a temporary array the element moves out instead. `void[]` has no `at`.

Ownership: borrows the array; scalar elements are copied.

Returns the first element for which `callback` returns `true`, or `undefined`. Searches from the start, with original indices, and stops at the first match; an empty array invokes no callbacks. The array is not changed. The callback is a strict-boolean predicate: it must return a boolean.

@param callback - Exactly one reusable callback, with no default; a strict-boolean, boolean-returning predicate (no truthiness conversion).

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [1, 2, 3];
const selected = values.find((value: int, index: int, source: int[]): boolean => {
  return value > 1 && source[index] === value;
});
console.log(String(selected), values.join("|")); // 2 1|2|3
let calls: int = 0;
const empty: int[] = [];
console.log(String(empty.find((): boolean => {
  calls += 1;
  return true;
})), calls);
const missing: int[] = [1, 2, 3];
console.log(String(missing.find((value: int): boolean => {
  calls += 1;
  return value > 9;
})), calls);
// undefined 0
// undefined 3
```

The callback receives each element, its index and the array; the callback may declare fewer parameters. Elements are lent to it read-only. For an array of functions it can call each one, which takes exclusive receiver access. An error thrown in the callback stops the loop and propagates. Extra optional parameters receive `undefined`; a rest parameter receives a fresh owning array, empty after these three arguments.

```tsrust
const values = [3, 1, 2];
console.log(values.find((value: int, index: int, source: ReadOnly<int[]>, extra: int = 2): boolean => {
  return value === extra;
}) ?? -1);
// 2
```

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [2, 3];
console.log(String(values.find((value: int, index: int, source: int[], ...tail: int[]): boolean => {
  return value > 2 && tail.length === 0;
})));
// 3
```

On a named array of strings, arrays or records, the result is a scoped read-only view: the array cannot change while it is used, and `clone(view)` makes an owned copy. On a temporary array the element moves out instead.

Ownership: borrows the array; scalar results are copied.

