Ownership: borrows the array; scalar results are copied.

Adds zero or more `values` to the front of the array, in argument order, and returns the new int length. With no arguments it returns the current length, changes nothing and needs only read access.

@param values - Zero or more elements of exactly the element type. Arrays are inserted as elements, not flattened.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [3, 4];
console.log(values.unshift(1, 2), values.join("|")); // 4 1|2|3|4
const empty: int[] = [];
console.log(empty.unshift(), empty.length);
const single: int[] = [7];
console.log(single.unshift(), single.join("|"));
// 0 0
// 1 7
```

The array and then each argument are evaluated once, left to right, before the outer insertion. The new length must fit int. Adding elements needs mutable receiver access; existing elements move to higher indices.

Ownership: owned arguments move into the array without cloning; an element read from another array needs an explicit `clone()`.

Removes `deleteCount` elements starting at `start`, inserts `items` there, and returns the removed elements as an independent, mutable array. With no arguments it returns an empty array and changes nothing.

@param start - Index to start at; negative counts from the end. Clamped to the array. Accepts the full signed 64-bit range.
@param deleteCount - How many to remove. With only start, removes the rest of the array; zero removes nothing (so omitting it differs from passing 0); negative removes nothing. Explicit undefined is an error.
@param items - Elements to insert, of exactly the element type, inserted without flattening.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const removed = values.splice(1, 2, 8, 9);
console.log(removed.join("|"), values.join("|")); // 2|3 1|8|9|4
```

All arguments are evaluated once, left to right, before the outer splice; positions use the post-argument current length. Any change needs mutable receiver access. Arrays of functions are supported: removed functions keep their captured state.

Ownership: removed elements move into the result and inserted ones into the array, without implicit cloning; reading an element from elsewhere needs explicit `clone()`. Read access suffices with no arguments.

Sorts the array in place and returns nothing. The sort is stable: equal elements keep their order.

@param compare - Optional callback returning int or number: negative puts `left` first, positive puts it after, zero or `NaN` keeps the order. It may declare fewer parameters and cannot change the array.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [10, 2, -1, 2];
values.sort();
console.log(values.join("|"));
// -1|2|2|10
```

Without `compare`: ints sort numerically, numbers by total order (`-0` before `0`, `NaN` by its bits), `false` before `true`, and strings by UTF-8 byte order. Unlike JavaScript, numbers are not compared as text. Records, nested arrays and functions need a comparator, even when empty. The invocation order and count of `compare` follow the Rust sort.

Requires mutable receiver access. Elements are rearranged without cloning. An error thrown by `compare` leaves the array in its original order.

```tsrust
const values = [3, 1, 2];
values.sort((left?: int, right?: int, extra: int = 0): int => {
  return (left ?? 0) - (right ?? 0) + extra;
});
console.log(values[0], values[1], values[2]);
// 1 2 3
```

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [3, 1, 2];
values.sort((left: int, ...tail: int[]): int => {
  return left - tail[0];
});
console.log(values.join(","));
// 1,2,3
```

Ownership: the array keeps its elements; nothing is copied.

