Returns an independently mutable array with nested arrays flattened into it, in order. The array is unchanged. The receiver is evaluated once.

@param depth - How many levels to flatten, default 1; 0 or less makes a copy without flattening. On a nested array it must be a signed integer literal (the result type depends on it). `number` values and explicit undefined are errors.

Empty nested arrays add nothing.

```tsrust
const values: int[][] = [[1, 2], [], [3]];
const flattened = values.flat();
console.log(flattened.join("|"), values.length);
// prints 1|2|3 3
```

Ownership: borrows the array and copies its elements into the result; a temporary array moves them instead.

Calls `callback` for each element and returns its results in one independently mutable array. When the callback returns an array, its elements are added (one level only). The source is unchanged. The callback runs once per element.

@param callback - Returns a value or an array of values, of one fixed concrete type.

Empty returned arrays add nothing.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [2, 3];
const mapped = values.flatMap((value: int, index: int, source: int[]): int => {
  return value + index + source.length;
});
console.log(mapped.join("|"), values.join("|"));
// prints 4|6 2|3
```

The callback receives each element, its index and the array; the callback may declare fewer parameters. Elements are lent to it read-only. For an array of functions it can call each one, which takes exclusive receiver access. An error thrown in the callback stops the loop and propagates. Extra optional parameters receive `undefined`; a rest parameter receives a fresh owning array, empty after these three arguments.

```tsrust
const values = [3, 1, 2];
const flat = values.flatMap((value: int, index: int, source: ReadOnly<int[]>, extra: int = 5): int[] => {
  return [value + extra];
});
console.log(flat[0], flat[1], flat[2]);
// 8 6 7
```

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [2, 3];
const result = values.flatMap((value: int, index: int, source: int[], ...tail: int[]): int[] => {
  return [value + tail.length];
});
console.log(result.join(","));
// 2,3
```

Ownership: borrows the source for Read access; values the callback returns move into the result. Returning an element of the source needs explicit `clone()`.

