Array.documentation:
Returns an array of `[index, value]` pairs, in order. Takes no arguments; passing one is an arity error. An empty array gives an empty result, and the array is unchanged. The pairs are built eagerly, not lazily.

```tsrust
const words = ["cat", "dog"];
const pairs = words.entries();
console.log(pairs[0][0], pairs[0][1], pairs[1][0]);
// 0 cat 1
pairs[0][1] = "changed";
console.log(words[0], pairs[0][1]);
// cat changed
```

```tsrust
let sum = 0;

for (const pair of [4, 5].entries()) {
  sum += pair[0] + pair[1];
}

const empty: int[] = [];
console.log(sum, empty.entries().length);
// 10 0
```

The result is an ordinary array (use it with `for...of`, `Array.from` or array methods), built when called: later changes to the source do not show up in it. Arrays holding functions cannot produce entries, nor can `void[]`.

Ownership: copies the elements into the pairs; the caller owns the result.

Returns the index of every element, in order: `0`, `1`, up to `length - 1`. Takes no arguments. An empty array gives an empty result.

```tsrust
const words = ["a", "b", "c"];
console.log(words.keys().join(",")); // prints "0,1,2"
```

```tsrust
const words = ["a", "b"];
let total = 0;

for (const index of words.keys()) {
  total += index;
}

const empty: int[] = [];
console.log(total, empty.keys().length); // prints "1 0"
```

The result is an ordinary `int[]`, so it works in `for...of`, with `Array.from`, in array spread and with every array method. Passing an argument is an `arity` error. It is a new array computed when called; it does not follow later changes to the receiver, and there is no iterator object with a `next()` method.

Receiver is borrowed and unchanged; the returned array is owned by the caller.

Returns the elements in order as a new array. Takes no arguments. An empty array gives an empty result.

```tsrust
const words = ["a", "b"];
const copy = words.values();
copy[0] = "changed";
console.log(words.join(","), copy.join(",")); // prints "a,b changed,b"
```

```tsrust
let total = 0;

for (const value of [1, 2, 3].values()) {
  total += value;
}

console.log(total); // prints "6"
```

The result is an ordinary array of the same element type, so it works in `for...of`, with `Array.from`, in array spread and with every array method. Passing an argument is an `arity` error. It is an independent copy made when called: changing it never changes the receiver, it does not follow later changes to the receiver, and there is no iterator object with a `next()` method.

Receiver is borrowed and unchanged; owned elements such as strings and records are copied into the returned array.

Number.documentation:
Not-a-number value. Equality with itself is false; use Number.isNaN to test it. Read-only number constant; takes no arguments.

```tsrust
function main() {
  const value = Number.NaN;
  console.log(value === value, Number.isNaN(value), value + 1);
}
// prints: false true NaN
```

Constants are primitive copies and allocate nothing.

JSON.documentation:
Parses a JSON document into a `JsonValue`, or throws a `JsonError`.

Accepts exactly one strict JSON document (UTF-8, surrounding whitespace allowed). Trailing data, comments, bad escapes, lone surrogates and non-finite numbers are rejected. Duplicate keys keep the last value; object keys are kept sorted; integers stay exact within 64 bits (larger ones fail with `integer-range`); `-0` survives. Without limits only nesting is bounded (128 levels); pass `JsonLimits` to cap bytes, depth, entries and total values for untrusted input. Errors carry a `code`, `message` and byte `offset`. Catch errors with `try`/`catch`.

```tsrust
function main() {
  try {
    JSON.parse("null secret");
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.code, error.offset);
  }
}
```

With a type argument (`parse<Item>(text)`), it validates and builds `Item` directly: required fields must be present, optional ones become `undefined`, unknown fields are skipped, and a wrong shape fails with `shape-mismatch`. `int` needs an integral value in signed 64-bit range; `number` rejects integers that would lose precision (`number-precision`). Literal types and unions of literals are checked exactly. This is checked decoding, not a type assertion. For example, with `type Item = { name: string; count: int }`, `count: 2.5` fails instead of truncating. A `JsonRaw` field keeps that value's original text without decoding it (the source text must outlive the result); JSON.stringify and JSON.tryStringify preserve raw source text exactly.

Ownership: borrows `text` and `limits`; the result owns its data and can outlive the input (except `JsonRaw` fields).

Parses a JSON document, returning `Ok(JsonValue)` or `Err(JsonError)` instead of throwing.

Accepts exactly one strict JSON document (UTF-8, surrounding whitespace allowed). Trailing data, comments, bad escapes, lone surrogates and non-finite numbers are rejected. Duplicate keys keep the last value; object keys are kept sorted; integers stay exact within 64 bits (larger ones fail with `integer-range`); `-0` survives. Without limits only nesting is bounded (128 levels); pass `JsonLimits` to cap bytes, depth, entries and total values for untrusted input. Errors carry a `code`, `message` and byte `offset`. Inspect the result with `match`.

```tsrust
function main() {
  match (JSON.tryParse("null secret")) {
    Ok(_) => {
      throw "unexpected success";
    },
    Err(error) => {
      console.log(error.code, error.offset);
    }
  }
}
```

With a type argument (`parse<Item>(text)`), it validates and builds `Item` directly: required fields must be present, optional ones become `undefined`, unknown fields are skipped, and a wrong shape fails with `shape-mismatch`. `int` needs an integral value in signed 64-bit range; `number` rejects integers that would lose precision (`number-precision`). Literal types and unions of literals are checked exactly. This is checked decoding, not a type assertion. A `JsonRaw` field keeps that value's original text without decoding it (the source text must outlive the result); JSON.stringify and JSON.tryStringify preserve raw source text exactly.

Ownership: borrows `text` and `limits`; the result owns its data and can outlive the input (except `JsonRaw` fields).

Encodes a value (scalars, records, tuples, arrays or JSON values) as strict JSON text, or throws a `JsonError`. `JsonRaw` values are written with their original source text exactly. For one record per line, use JSON Lines (`writeJsonl`).

Keys are sorted, tuples become arrays, `undefined` object fields are omitted, and ints and `-0` are exact. Fails with `undefined-value` for an `undefined` root or array element, `number-nonfinite` for `NaN`/`Infinity`, and `output-limit` when `maxBytes` is exceeded (no partial output). Omitting `maxBytes` means no output byte quota; with it, the output byte limit applies; there is no container-depth quota. Quotes, backslashes and control characters are escaped; other Unicode stays as UTF-8.

```tsrust
type Item = {
  name: string;
  count: int;
};

function main() {
  const item: Item = {
    name: "hello", count: 42
  };
  console.log(JSON.stringify(item));
}
```

Resources, collections and functions are rejected at compile time; there is no replacer callback.

Ownership: borrows the value; returns an independently owned string.

Encodes a value as strict JSON text and returns Result<string, JsonError> instead of throwing. `JsonRaw` values are written with their original source text exactly. For one record per line, use JSON Lines (`writeJsonl`).

Keys are sorted, tuples become arrays, `undefined` object fields are omitted, and ints and `-0` are exact. Fails with `undefined-value` for an `undefined` root or array element, `number-nonfinite` for `NaN`/`Infinity`, and `output-limit` when `maxBytes` is exceeded (no partial output). Omitting `maxBytes` means no output byte quota; with it, the output byte limit applies; there is no container-depth quota.

```tsrust
function main() {
  match (JSON.tryStringify(undefined)) {
    Ok(_) => {
      throw "unexpected success";
    },
    Err(error) => {
      console.log(error.code);
    }
  }
}
```

Resources, collections and functions are rejected at compile time.

Ownership: borrows the value; returns an independently owned string.

console.documentation:
Prints `data` as a table on stdout. Array indices or record keys label the rows; the fields of each row become columns, and primitive rows go in a `Values` column. A primitive `data` prints normally; no argument prints `undefined`.

@param data - Array or record to tabulate.
@param properties - Optional column names, in order. Unknown names give empty columns; an empty list shows only the row labels. Omitted, columns come from the rows.

```tsrust
console.table({
  name: "Pat", age: 7
});
// Columns: (index), Values; rows: name -> 'Pat', age -> 7.
```

Nullable values print their present payload, `null`, or `undefined`.

Ownership: borrows `data`.

Prints one value on stdout with inspection; strings are not treated as format strings. No argument prints `undefined`.

@param value - Value to inspect.
@param options - `depth` (default 2; `null` for the maximum, 128; -1 hides contents), `showHidden` (default false; adds array length) and `colors` (default false; ANSI colors). Other fields are ignored.

```tsrust
console.dir({
  a: {
    b: 1
  }
}, {
  depth: 0
}); // stdout: { a: [Object] }
```

Nullable values print their present payload, `null`, or `undefined`. Functions and mixed-type arrays are not supported.

Ownership: borrows the value.

Prints an optional formatted label, then indents subsequent stdout and stderr console lines by two spaces. Collapsed groups display the same way as normal groups. Nested calls add indentation; groupEnd removes one level.

@param values - Zero or more values forming a log-style label; omission prints no label.

```tsrust
console.group("work");
console.log(1);
console.groupEnd();
// stdout: work, then two spaces followed by 1.
```

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Prints an optional formatted label, then indents subsequent stdout and stderr console lines by two spaces. Collapsed groups display the same way as normal groups. Nested calls add indentation; groupEnd removes one level.

@param values - Zero or more values forming a log-style label; omission prints no label.

```tsrust
console.groupCollapsed("work");
console.log(1);
console.groupEnd();
// stdout: work, then two spaces followed by 1.
```

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Resets the counter for `label` (default "default"). Prints nothing on success; a missing label writes a warning to stderr. Non-string labels other than undefined warn.

@param label - Counter label. Non-string labels warn and change nothing, even if `count` created a counter with that text: after `count(1)`, reset it with `countReset("1")`.

```tsrust
function main() {
  console.count("jobs");
  console.count("jobs");
  console.countReset("jobs");
  console.count("jobs");
}
// prints: jobs: 1
// prints: jobs: 2
// prints: jobs: 1
```

Ownership: borrows the label.

Starts a monotonic timer identified by label. Writes nothing when successful; a duplicate label warns and keeps the existing timer.

@param label - Optional printable label, default "default" when omitted or undefined.

```tsrust
console.time("work");
console.timeLog("work", 7);
console.timeEnd("work");
// stdout: work: <elapsed>ms 7, then work: <elapsed>ms.
```

Use timers to measure how long a section of code takes. Durations are printed in milliseconds with up to three fractional digits. Missing labels write a warning immediately to stderr.

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Prints the elapsed duration without removing the timer, followed by optional additional inspected values. Message values do not perform format substitution.

@param label - Optional printable label, default "default" when omitted or undefined.
@param values - Optional extra printable values.

```tsrust
function main() {
  console.time("import");
  let rows = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    rows += 1;
  }

  console.timeLog("import", "rows:", rows); // import: <elapsed>ms rows: 1000
  console.timeEnd("import");
}
```

Use timers to measure how long a section of code takes. Durations are printed in milliseconds with up to three fractional digits. Missing labels write a warning immediately to stderr.

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Prints the elapsed duration and removes the timer.

@param label - Optional printable label, default "default" when omitted or undefined.

```tsrust
function main() {
  console.time();
  console.timeEnd(); // default: <elapsed>ms
  console.timeEnd(); // warning on stderr: the timer no longer exists
}
```

Use timers to measure how long a section of code takes. Durations are printed in milliseconds with up to three fractional digits. Missing labels write a warning immediately to stderr.

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

number.documentation:
Formats the number as English decimal text with a comma between groups of three digits and a period before the fraction, for example `1,234.5`. The output does not depend on the system language.

Fractions round to at most three decimal places (halves away from zero) and trailing zeros are dropped. Large numbers are written in full, never in exponent form. NaN gives `"NaN"`; infinities give `"∞"` and `"-∞"`; negative zero keeps its minus sign.

```tsrust
const value: number = 1234.5675;
const small: number = -0.0004;
console.log(value.toLocaleString(), small.toLocaleString()); // prints 1,234.568 -0
```

```tsrust
console.log(Number.NaN.toLocaleString(), Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY.toLocaleString(), Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY.toLocaleString());
// prints NaN ∞ -∞
```

Locale and options arguments are an `unsupported` error; remove them.

Ownership: returns a new string.

int.documentation:
Formats a value as decimal text with a comma between each group of three integer digits. Takes no arguments. The decimal separator is a period; formatting is fixed and does not depend on the system language.

Formats the full signed 64-bit integer range exactly. There is no rounding or exponent notation.

```tsrust
const value: int = -1234567;
console.log(value.toLocaleString(), value); // prints -1,234,567 -1234567
```

```tsrust
const value: int = 9223372036854775807;
console.log(value.toLocaleString());
// prints 9,223,372,036,854,775,807
```

Returns a new string and leaves the receiver unchanged. Zero gives `"0"`. Locale and options arguments are an `unsupported` error; remove them. Currency, alternate digits and grouping rules for other languages are unavailable. Integers outside the exact range of a floating-point number retain all their digits.

Ownership: the integer receiver is copied; the result owns its text.

globals.documentation:
Returns an independent copy of a string, array, record, `Set`, `Map`, `Bytes` or a union holding one. Nested values are copied too; the copy keeps the type and active union variant. Takes a Required cloneable owned value, preserving its type and active union variant; byte buffers get independent byte storage. For `Bytes` the copy gets independent byte storage; allocation failure is not recoverable.

@param value - Required cloneable owned value; it is borrowed without consuming it.

```tsrust
import { alloc, at, set } from "std:bytes";

function main() {
  match (alloc(1, 65)) {
    Ok(bytes) => {
      const other = clone(bytes);

      match (set(bytes, 0, 90)) {
        Ok(_) => {
          match (at(other, 0)) {
            Ok(value) => {
              console.log(value);
            },
            Err(error) => {
              console.log(error.message);
            }
          }
        },
        Err(error) => {
          console.log(error.message);
        }
      }
    },
    Err(error) => {
      console.log(error.message);
    }
  }
}
```

Writing 90 to the source leaves the clone unchanged. Scalars need no cloning; functions and most native resources cannot be cloned.

Ownership: borrows the source and returns a new owner. Assignment and passing by value still move values without cloning.

Holds either a success value (`Ok(value)`) or an error (`Err(error)`). Use it when the caller must handle failure explicitly.

Inspect it with an exhaustive `match`; a `match` can also produce a value: `const value = match (result) { Ok(value) => value, Err(error) => { throw error; }, };`. Missing variants are compile errors; a final `_` arm handles the rest. There are no `.value` or `.error` fields. A `void` variant is built and matched as `Ok()`/`Ok(_)`.

Matching a `ReadOnly<Result<T, E>>` parameter lends the payload for that arm; a `Mutable<Result<T, E>>` parameter lends it for in-place changes. Scalar payloads are copied into the binding.

```tsrust
const result: Result<int, string> = Ok(5);

match (result) {
  Ok(value) => {
    console.log(value);
  },
  Err(_) => {
    console.log(-1);
  }
}
// 5
```

```tsrust
const callback: Result<Function<int>, string> = Ok((): int => 7);

match (callback) {
  Ok(action) => {
    console.log(action());
  },
  Err(_) => {
    console.log(-1);
  }
}
// 7
```

```tsrust
function resultLength(result: ReadOnly<Result<string, int>>): int {
  match (result) {
    Ok(text) => {
      return text.length;
    },
    Err(code) => {
      return code;
    }
  }
}

const borrowed: Result<string, int> = Ok("abc");
console.log(resultLength(borrowed), resultLength(borrowed));
// 3 3
```

```tsrust
function append(result: Mutable<Result<int[], string>>): void {
  match (result) {
    Ok(values) => {
      values.push(9);
    },
    Err(_) => {}
  }
}

const editable: Result<int[], string> = Ok([1]);
append(editable);
append(editable);

match (editable) {
  Ok(values) => {
    console.log(values.length);
  },
  Err(_) => {}
}
// 3
```

Ownership: owned payloads move into the Result without an implicit clone; matching an owned Result moves the payload into its arm.

Constructs a successful Result containing value. A Result<T, E> annotation, call/return context, or explicit type arguments must determine both payload types. For example, use it to return a computed value from a fallible function. Takes exactly one value of type T; when T is void, takes no argument. A wrong payload type or argument count is a compile error. No error payload is created, and no exception is thrown. Use Ok<T, E>(...) when no surrounding Result type supplies that information.

```tsrust
const result = Ok<int, string>(5);

match (result) {
  Ok(value) => {
    console.log(value);
  },
  Err(_) => {
    console.log(-1);
  }
}
// 5
```

Ownership: Owned payloads transfer into the Result without an implicit clone. Matching an owned Result transfers it and exposes the selected payload in its arm; that binding does not escape its lexical scope unless explicitly returned or transferred.

Constructs a failed Result containing error. A Result<T, E> annotation, call/return context, or explicit type arguments must determine both payload types. Use it to return an error that the caller must inspect or forward. Takes exactly one error of type E; when E is void, takes no argument. A wrong payload type or argument count is a compile error. No success payload is created, and construction does not throw. Use Err<T, E>(...) when no surrounding Result type supplies that information.

```tsrust
const result = Err<int, string>("failed");

match (result) {
  Ok(_) => {
    console.log("unexpected");
  },
  Err(error) => {
    console.log(error);
  }
}
// failed
```

Ownership: Owned payloads transfer into the Result without an implicit clone. Matching an owned Result transfers it and exposes the selected payload in its arm; that binding does not escape its lexical scope unless explicitly returned or transferred.

An explicitly empty value. `null` has its own type, distinct from `undefined` and from the `void` result of a procedure. Use it in annotations and unions such as `string | null`, in record fields, and in arrays such as `null[]` or `(int | null)[]`.

`value === null` is true only for `null`, and also for a `JsonValue` holding JSON null (JSON null stays distinct from `undefined`). After a strict `=== null` or `!== null` check, a stable named binding or field loses or keeps the `null` alternative in that branch. Member access and arithmetic on `null` are compile errors. `typeof null` is `"object"`, as in JavaScript.

```tsrust
function find(id: int): string | null {
  return id === 1 ? "Ada" : null;
}

function main() {
  const names: (string | null)[] = [find(1), find(2)];

  for (const name of names) {
    if (name === null) {
      console.log("missing");
    } else {
      console.log(name.toUpperCase()); // narrowed to string
    }
  }

  console.log(typeof null, Number(null));
}
// prints: ADA
// prints: missing
// prints: object 0
```

For optional chaining (`?.`) and fallbacks (`??`), see the Optional values chapter of the language reference.

Ownership: `null` owns nothing and copies freely; owned alternatives in a union keep their ordinary ownership rules.

The missing-value constant. `undefined` is distinct from `null` and from `void`, the unusable result type of a procedure that returns nothing. Use it in annotations and unions such as `int | undefined`, in record fields, in arrays such as `(int | undefined)[]`, and as the value of an omitted optional parameter.

After a strict `=== undefined` or `!== undefined` check, a stable named binding or field loses or keeps the `undefined` alternative in that branch. A local variable or parameter may shadow the global name; using a binding before its declaration is an error.

```tsrust
function lookup(key: string): int | undefined {
  return key === "port" ? 8080 : undefined;
}

function main() {
  const values: (int | undefined)[] = [lookup("port"), lookup("host")];
  console.log(values[0] ?? 0, values[1] ?? 0);
  const missing = lookup("host");
  console.log(missing === undefined, String(missing), Number(missing));
}
// prints: 8080 0
// prints: true undefined NaN
```

`console.log` prints `undefined`. `JSON.stringify` omits record fields that are `undefined` and rejects an `undefined` root value or array element with `undefined-value`; the console `%j` placeholder writes such array elements as `null`.

Ownership: `undefined` owns nothing and copies freely.

Returns the type category of a value as a string. This is a unary operator, not a function. The operand is evaluated once and is not consumed.

| Value | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `int` or `number` | `"number"` |
| `string` | `"string"` |
| `boolean` | `"boolean"` |
| `undefined`, or the result of a void call | `"undefined"` |
| `null`, arrays, tuples and records | `"object"` |
| functions | `"function"` |
| `JsonValue` | the category of the JSON data it holds; JSON null, arrays and objects give `"object"` |

For a union, `typeof` reports the alternative the value currently holds. Comparing it with a string literal, as in `typeof value === "string"` or `typeof value !== "boolean"`, narrows a stable named binding or record field inside that branch. Reassigning or mutating the place ends the narrowing. A computed array element is not a stable place; bind it to a `const` first. Because `"object"` covers null, arrays and records, it cannot tell them apart: test `value === null` or use `Array.isArray`.

On a `JsonValue`, a guard unlocks the payload: after `typeof value === "boolean"` the value can be copied into a `boolean`; after `typeof value === "string"` it supports borrowed reads (`length`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `includes`, `indexOf`, `lastIndexOf`), and `String(value)` makes an owned copy. Numbers stay JSON values; read them with `asInt` or `asNumber` from `std:json`, which reject precision loss.

```tsrust
import { asInt } from "std:json";

function describe(value: int | string | boolean): string {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    return value.toUpperCase(); // narrowed to string here
  }

  return typeof value;
}

function main() {
  console.log(
    typeof 1,
    typeof 1.5,
    typeof "a",
    typeof null,
    typeof[1],
    typeof undefined
  );
  console.log(describe("text"), describe(7), describe(true));
  const count = JSON.parse("3");

  if (typeof count === "number") {
    console.log(unwrap(asInt(count)) + 1); // read JSON numbers with asInt or asNumber
  }
}
// prints: number number string object object undefined
// prints: TEXT number boolean
// prints: 4
```

`int` and `number` both report `"number"`; `typeof` cannot distinguish them. An undeclared bare name gives `"undefined"`, but using a variable before its declaration is still an error. `console` and `Math` report `"object"`; `Number` and `clone` report `"function"`.

Ownership: the operand is borrowed; the result is static text.

Evaluates an expression exactly once, discards its result and returns undefined. This unary operator differs from the void procedure return annotation: a void call alone cannot be stored, but void call() produces a usable undefined value. It does not throw away type errors or skip effects.

@param expression - Expression evaluated for its effects; owned named inputs are borrowed, not moved.

```tsrust
let count = 0;
const missing: undefined = void count++;
console.log(count, missing); // 1 undefined
```

Does not enable dynamic coercion. The void result type cannot be a union member; the value undefined can. Temporary results are disposed after evaluation; the result is a zero-sized copy value.

Work that produces a value when awaited. Calling an async function creates a task; its arguments are evaluated immediately, but the body runs only when you `await` it or call `task.start()` to run it concurrently. `T` is the result type (`void` for none).

Errors from the body reach `try`/`catch` at the `await`. `task.timeout(ms)` adds a deadline. Timers and task groups are in `std:futures`.

```tsrust
async function answer(): Task<int> {
  return 42;
}

async function main(): Task<void> {
  const task = answer();
  console.log(await task); // 42
}
```

A task that is never awaited or started does not run; unused tasks produce a warning.

Ownership: owned arguments move into the task when it is created. A task cannot be cloned or awaited twice; borrowed arguments stay borrowed until it finishes.

The type of a function value: a named function, an arrow function, or a callable stored in a variable, field or array.

`Result` is the type the function returns; use `void` when it returns nothing. `[Arguments]` is an optional list of argument types in call order. Leaving it out means the function takes no arguments, so `Function<int>` is called as `action()`. An argument can have a label (`text: string`), be optional (`limit?: int`), or collect the remaining values (`...rest: int[]`). Labels do not change the type; they appear in parameter help. A required argument cannot follow an optional one, and a rest argument must be last.

```tsrust
const double: Function<int, [int]> = (value: int): int => {
  return value * 2;
};
console.log(double(4)); // prints "8"
```

```tsrust
const pick: Function<int, [first: int, second?: int, ...rest: int[]]> = (first: int, second?: int, ...rest: int[]): int => {
  return first + rest.length;
};
console.log(pick(1), pick(1, 2, 3, 4)); // prints "1 3"
```

An argument that owns data (a string, array or record) must say how the function uses it: `ReadOnly<T>`, `Mutable<T>` or `Owned<T>`. `Function<int, [string]>` is a `callback-access` error; write `Function<int, [ReadOnly<string>]>`. Scalars such as `int` are copied and take no wrapper. `Function` with no result type is a `type` error, and arguments not written as a list, such as `Function<int, string>`, are a `syntax` error.

This is an exact signature, not the loose JavaScript `Function` type: argument count and types are checked at every call.

Ownership: a plain `Function<...>` owns its captured values. Passing or assigning it transfers the function, and it can be called many times. Wrap it in `ReadOnly`, `Mutable` or `Once` to borrow it or to make it single-use.

Lends something for the duration of a call and allows it to be changed.

`T` is one type. Around a function type, `Mutable<Function<...>>` borrows a callback that may change the values it captured; the caller keeps the callback and can pass it again afterwards. Around an argument inside a `Function` argument list, `Mutable<T>` means the function changes the caller's string, array or record in place.

```tsrust
let calls = 0;
const count: Mutable<Function<int>> = (): int => {
  calls += 1;
  return calls;
};
console.log(count(), count()); // prints "1 2"
```

```tsrust
const values = [1];
const grow: ReadOnly<Function<int, [Mutable<int[]>]>> = (list: int[]): int => {
  list.push(2);
  return list.length;
};
console.log(grow(values), values.join(",")); // prints "2 1,2"
```

A borrowed callback cannot be returned, stored in a record or array, or captured by a returned function; that is a `callback-escape` error. Passing the same value as two `Mutable` arguments of one call is a `conflicting-access` error. `Mutable` around a scalar such as `int`, or around a data type outside a `Function` argument list, is a `callback-access` error. Wrappers do not nest: `ReadOnly<Mutable<T>>` is rejected.

Ownership: nothing is copied or moved. The caller remains the owner. A synchronous loan ends when the call returns; an async argument loan stays active until its Task completes or is disposed.

examples.source:
let calls = 0;
const count: Mutable<Function<int>> = (): int => { calls += 1; return calls; };
console.log(count(), count());

const values = [1];
const grow: ReadOnly<Function<int, [Mutable<int[]>]>> = (list: int[]): int => { list.push(2); return list.length; };
console.log(grow(values), values.join(","));

globals.documentation:
Lends something for the duration of a call without allowing it to be changed.

`T` is one type. Around a function type, `ReadOnly<Function<...>>` borrows a callback that only reads the values it captured; it can be called any number of times and the caller keeps it. Around an argument inside a `Function` argument list, `ReadOnly<T>` means the function reads the caller's string, array or record and leaves it unchanged.

```tsrust
const text = "abc";
const size: ReadOnly<Function<int>> = (): int => {
  return text.length;
};
console.log(size(), size(), text); // prints "3 3 abc"
```

```tsrust
const measure: ReadOnly<Function<int, [ReadOnly<string>]>> = (text: string): int => {
  return text.length;
};
const word = "four";
console.log(measure(word), word); // prints "4 four"
```

A callback that changes or consumes a captured value does not fit `ReadOnly<Function<...>>` and is a `callback-access` error. A borrowed callback cannot be returned or stored (`callback-escape`). `ReadOnly` around a scalar such as `int`, or around a data type outside a `Function` argument list, is a `callback-access` error.

This describes borrowing for one call. It is not TypeScript's `Readonly<T>`, which makes object properties read-only.

Ownership: nothing is copied or moved. The caller remains the owner. An async argument loan stays active until its Task completes or is disposed.

examples.source:
const text = "abc";
const size: ReadOnly<Function<int>> = (): int => { return text.length; };
console.log(size(), size(), text);

const measure: ReadOnly<Function<int, [ReadOnly<string>]>> = (text: string): int => { return text.length; };
const word = "four";
console.log(measure(word), word);

globals.documentation:
A function value that can be called exactly one time.

`T` must be a function type, as in `Once<Function<string>>`. The single call may move the values the function captured out of it, for example returning a captured string without copying it.

```tsrust
const label = "done".repeat(1);
const finish: Once<Function<string>> = (): string => {
  return label;
};
console.log(finish()); // prints "done"
```

Calling the same `Once` function a second time is a `closure-consumed` error for a local value and a `callback-consumed` error for a parameter. `Once` around anything other than a function type, such as `Once<int>`, is a `callback-access` error; use `Owned<T>` for a data argument.

Ownership: the function is owned. Passing it transfers it, and calling it uses it up.

examples.source:
const label = "done".repeat(1);
const finish: Once<Function<string>> = (): string => { return label; };
console.log(finish());

globals.documentation:
Marks an argument that the function takes over, or spells out that a function value is owned.

`T` is one type. Around an argument inside a `Function` argument list, `Owned<T>` means the caller gives the string, array or record to the function; the function can keep it, return it or let it go. Around a function type, `Owned<Function<...>>` means the same as plain `Function<...>`.

```tsrust
const keep: ReadOnly<Function<string, [Owned<string>]>> = (text: string): string => {
  return text;
};
console.log(keep("moved".repeat(1))); // prints "moved"
```

After a value is passed as an `Owned` argument the caller can no longer use it; a later use is a `transferred` error. Pass `clone(value)` to keep the original. `Owned` around a scalar such as `int`, or around a data type outside a `Function` argument list, is a `callback-access` error.

Ownership: the value moves into the function without copying.

Tests whether a number is `NaN`, the result of an undefined numeric operation such as `0.0 / 0.0` or of reading a number from text that has none. It is the same function as `Number.isNaN`.

@param value - Number to test. An int is accepted and is never `NaN`.

@returns `true` only for `NaN`; `false` for every other number, including the infinities.

```tsrust
const parsed = parseInt("abc");
console.log(isNaN(parsed), isNaN(1.5), isNaN(Infinity), parsed === NaN); // prints "true false false false"
```

Use this function instead of `value === NaN`, which is always `false`. Exactly one number is required: another count is an `arity` error, and a string, boolean or other type is a `type` error because no value is converted to a number first; convert explicitly with `Number(value)`. A function or variable that you declare with the name `isNaN` takes precedence.

The number is copied; nothing is changed or allocated.

The positive infinite number, the same value as `Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY`. It is greater than every other number. Write `-Infinity` for the negative infinite number. It takes no arguments.

```tsrust
let lowest = Infinity;

for (const value of [4.5, 2.5, 8.0]) {
  if (value < lowest) {
    lowest = value;
  }
}

console.log(lowest, Infinity > Number.MAX_VALUE, -Infinity, isFinite(Infinity)); // prints "2.5 true -Infinity false"
```

A common use is the starting value of a running minimum (`Infinity`) or maximum (`-Infinity`). `isFinite(Infinity)` is `false`. `Infinity - Infinity` and `Infinity * 0.0` give `NaN`. Its type is `number`; assigning it to an `int` is a `type` error. It cannot be assigned to. A variable or parameter that you declare with the name `Infinity` takes precedence.

The value is a plain copied number.

Creates a map of keys to values that remembers insertion order.

@param source - Optional `[key, value]` pairs or another Map to copy. `null` or `undefined` gives an empty map. Without a source, give the types: `new Map<string, int>()`.

Setting an existing key replaces the value and keeps its position. Keys are scalars, enums or unions of them (records and arrays cannot be keys). Number keys follow `SameValueZero`: `NaN` equals `NaN`, and `0` and `-0` are the same key.

```tsrust
const values = new Map<string, int>();
values.set("a", 1);
console.log(values.size); // 1
```

Ownership: copies the source's keys and values; the source stays usable.

Returns the Ok payload or throws the original Err payload through ordinary try/catch/finally. Takes one Result, with no defaults or additional options. Result<void, E> returns no value on success. Errors retain their type and owned contents; unwrap does not panic or stringify them. Use match when each failure needs local recovery.

Ownership: consumes the Result and moves its active payload without cloning. Calls must be direct; polymorphic function values are unsupported.

```tsrust
function half(value: int): Result<int, string> {
  if (value % 2 !== 0) {
    return Err(`${value} is odd`);
  }

  return Ok(value / 2);
}

function main() {
  console.log(unwrap(half(8)));

  try {
    unwrap(half(3));
  } catch (error) {
    console.log("caught:", error); // the original Err payload, still a string
  }
}
// prints: 4
// prints: caught: 3 is odd
```

`import { unwrap } from "std:result"` refers to the same function and is kept for older code.

Declares that a function catches every failure in its own body and returns it as a value.

Write `Checked<T>` or `Checked<T, E>` as a function's declared result. Inside, the function is written as usual: `return value` returns a `T`. A runtime fault (integer overflow, an index out of range, a failed assertion, a length limit) anywhere in the body or in a function it calls, or a `throw` of an `E`, ends the call with an error instead of ending the program. Callers receive `Result<T, RuntimeError | E>` and use it with `match`, `unwrap` or `try`/`catch` like any other `Result`.

`T` is the success type; `Checked<void>` functions return `Ok()` when they finish. `E` is optional and lists the explicit errors the body may throw or pass on with `unwrap`; `RuntimeError` is always included.

```tsrust
function parse(text: string): Checked<int, string> {
  if (text.length === 0) {
    throw 'empty';
  }

  return text.length * 4611686018427387904;
}
// parse('a') is Ok(4611686018427387904)
// parse('') is Err('empty')
// parse('ab') is Err with a RuntimeError: integer multiplication overflow
```

Async functions declare `Task<Checked<T, E>>`. A function type may return `Checked<T, E>`, as in `Function<Checked<int>>`. A body that can throw or unwrap an error not listed in `E` is a `checked` error naming the missing type. `Checked` is rejected on `main`, on parameters, fields and variables, and inside another `Checked`.

Use it at the edges where a failure should be handled: request handlers, input parsing, a retry loop. Elsewhere, faults end the program with a report, and ordinary calls carry no error bookkeeping.

Ownership: no values are copied. The success value and the error move to the caller; values owned by the body are released when it fails.

examples.source:
function parse(text: string): Checked<int, string> {
  if (text.length === 0) {
    throw 'empty';
  }
  return text.length * 4611686018427387904;
}

function main() {
  for (const text of ['a', '', 'ab']) {
    match (parse(text)) {
      Ok(value) => console.log(value),
      Err(_) => console.log('failed'),
    }
  }
}

function scale(value: int): Checked<int> {
  return value * 4611686018427387904;
}

function main() {
  match (scale(2)) {
    Ok(value) => console.log(value),
    Err(error) => console.log(error.name, error.code, error.line, error.column),
  }
}

ArrayConstructor.documentation:
Returns `true` if the value is an array or tuple, otherwise `false` (including for no argument). A `JsonValue` returns `true` when it holds a JSON array. A union checks its current alternative. JSON arrays return true.

@param value - Value to test.

```tsrust
console.log(Array.isArray([]), Array.isArray({
  a: 1
}), Array.isArray(undefined)); // true false false
```

Used in a condition on a variable or field, it narrows a static union to its array alternatives (and the rest on the false branch). A `JsonValue` stays a `JsonValue`: its elements are still checked when read.

```tsrust
function main() {
  const value = JSON.parse("[null]");
  console.log(Array.isArray(value), value === null, JSON.parse("null") === null);
}
```

Expected output: true false true. JSON arrays may contain mixed JSON kinds; inspection does not grant unchecked element access.

Creates a dense, homogeneous array containing its arguments in their original order. Unlike a length constructor, Array.of(3) creates one element whose value is 3. Takes zero or more items and leaves the result independently mutable.

@param items - Variadic elements with one exact supported element type T. T may be inferred from nonempty arguments, supplied by an expected T[] result, or given explicitly as Array.of<T>(...). A typed or contextual empty call is accepted; an empty call without either type source is rejected. Explicit calls take exactly one type argument. Contextual Number elements accept integer literals, but typed int and Number values are not implicitly converted. Arguments evaluate once from left to right; completed argument effects remain if a later argument fails.

```tsrust
const values = Array.of (3, 1, 2);
console.log(values.join("|"));
// prints 3|1|2
```

Supports dense scalar, string, nested-array, record, tuple, union and owning stored-callable elements. Contextual union elements retain their declared alternatives. Homogeneous null[] and undefined[] results are supported, for example `Array.of(null, null)`. Sparse slots and JavaScript coercion are unsupported. The result array rank is at most 128, so a nested-array T has rank at most 127; generic record arguments obey the same type limits. Native lengths and ordinary Rust capacity limits apply; allocation can fail. This is a typed constructor, not a callable general Array constructor or a prototype/subclass hook.

Owned named String, array and record arguments transfer into the new array; they are not implicitly cloned. Borrowed indexed owners and scoped read-only views need an explicit clone before transfer. Copy elements are copied; the returned array owns its elements.

Spread arguments accept arrays, strings, Sets and Maps, for example `Array.of(0, ...new Set<int>([2, 1]), 3)`. Each source evaluates once and its elements are staged before the next argument evaluates. Strings yield Unicode scalar character strings; a combining mark is a separate element. Sets yield insertion-ordered values; Maps yield insertion-ordered key/value tuples. Scalar elements copy. Owned collection elements and Map tuples transfer, including noncloneable values; readonly owners require explicit copying before transfer. String spread constructs character values and leaves its source usable. Empty Set/Map constructors can receive their element types from the expected array result. Array literals use the same rules: `[0, ...values, 3]`. Arbitrary iterator objects remain unsupported.

Creates a new array from an array, the characters of a string (an emoji is one element), the values of a Set or the `[key, value]` tuples of a Map, in order. Empty sources give empty arrays. Array.from creates a `string[]` from a string.

@param source - Array, string, Set or Map.
@param map - Optional function called with `(item, index)` for each item (for a Map, the item is a readonly `[key, value]` tuple); its results form the array. It may take fewer parameters; there is no `thisArg`. Errors it throws stop the copy and propagate.

Without `map`, owned items are copied deeply (JavaScript would share nested objects), so they must be cloneable.

```tsrust
const source = [3, 1, 2];
const copy = Array.from(source);
copy.push(9);
console.log(source.join(","), copy.join(","));
// 3,1,2 3,1,2,9
```

```tsrust
console.log(Array.from(new Set<int>([3, 1, 3]), (value, index) => {
  return value * 10 + index;
}).join(","));
// 30,11
```

```tsrust
const values = new Map<string, int>([["a", 2], ["b", 3]]);
const pairs = Array.from(values);
pairs[0][1] = 9;
console.log(Array.from(values, ([key, value], index) => {
  return key + String(value + index);
}).join("|"), pairs[0][1]);
// a2|b4 9
```

```tsrust
console.log(Array.from("aé😀").join("|"), Array.from("").length);
// a|é|😀 0
```

Iterator objects and array-like records with `length` are unsupported.

Ownership: borrows the source; the caller owns the new array. To keep a borrowed item from `map`, return `clone(item)`.

std:os.documentation:
Returns the CPU byte order for which the executable was compiled: BE for big endian or LE for little endian. Takes no arguments and has no defaults. The result is constant during execution, including execution under translation. It follows the executable compilation target, not physical hardware. An unsupported byte-order selector produces a native compilation error. No receiver or external state is mutated.

```tsrust
import { endianness } from "std:os";

function main() {
  const order = endianness();
  console.log(order === "BE" || order === "LE");
} // true
```

Ownership: returns an independent string value.

Returns the executable compilation target’s operating-system name. Takes no arguments and has no defaults. Returns one of aix, android, darwin, freebsd, linux, openbsd, sunos or win32; the result remains constant during execution. It describes the compiled executable, including a translated executable, rather than physical hardware. Unsupported target components produce a native compilation error. No receiver or external state is mutated.

```tsrust
import { platform } from 'std:os';

function main() {
  console.log(platform() === platform());
} // prints true
```

Ownership: returns an independent string value.

Returns the executable compilation target’s CPU architecture name. Takes no arguments and has no defaults. Returns one of arm, arm64, ia32, loong64, mips, mipsel, ppc64, riscv64, s390x or x64; the result remains constant during execution. It describes the compiled executable, including a translated executable, rather than physical hardware. Unsupported target components produce a native compilation error. No receiver or external state is mutated.

```tsrust
import { arch } from 'std:os';

function main() {
  console.log(arch() === arch());
} // prints true
```

Ownership: returns an independent string value.

An owned OS observation failure returned as Result data. Io reports a failed query, NotUnicode reports a host name that cannot be represented as Unicode, and Range reports a parallelism count outside int.

Ownership: owns its message; no rejected non-Unicode value is disclosed.

```tsrust
import { OsError } from 'std:os';

function main() {
  const error: OsError = {
    code: 'Io', message: 'observation unavailable'
  };
  console.log(error.code);
}
```

Expected output: Io.

Queries a positive estimate of the parallelism the running program can use. No arguments. The estimate may differ from CPU count and can change with resource limits; each call queries again. Does not account for all load or hardware topology, cache results, cap values or substitute one on failure. OS query failures return Io; an unrepresentable int count returns Range.

Ownership: no input is borrowed; Result owns its value or error.

```tsrust
import { availableParallelism } from 'std:os';

function main() {
  match (availableParallelism()) {
    Ok(value) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'Io' && error.code !== 'NotUnicode' && error.code !== 'Range') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('OS observation completed');
}
```

Expected output: OS observation completed.

Queries the OS host name as independently owned Unicode text. No arguments. Does not perform DNS lookup, read a configuration environment variable or change the host name. Returns Io on OS failure and NotUnicode without lossy replacement for an unrepresentable name. Windows reports the physical DNS host name; platform naming rules may differ.

Ownership: no input is borrowed; Result owns its value or error.

```tsrust
import { hostname } from 'std:os';

function main() {
  match (hostname()) {
    Ok(value) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'Io' && error.code !== 'NotUnicode' && error.code !== 'Range') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('OS observation completed');
}
```

Expected output: OS observation completed.

std:path.documentation:
Joins path segments with the platform separator (`/`, or `\` on Windows) and normalizes the result: `.` and `..` segments are resolved and empty segments are skipped. No segments returns `.`. A later absolute segment does not replace earlier ones. Windows drive and UNC prefixes are kept.

```tsrust
import { join } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(join("reports", "..", "archive"));
  console.log(join());
} // prints archive, then .
```

The filesystem is not read and symlinks are not resolved, so this is no containment check.

Ownership: borrows the segments; returns a new string.

Returns the last component of `path`, ignoring trailing separators. With `suffix`, also removes that ending (case-sensitive) unless it is the whole component. Works without reading the filesystem.

```tsrust
import { basename } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(basename("/tmp/report.txt")); // report.txt
  console.log(basename("/tmp/report.txt", ".txt")); // report
}
```

An empty path or a root returns "". `.` and `..` are returned as is. A partial suffix match can keep a trailing separator: basename("/foo/a/", "ba") returns "a/". Windows paths accept `/` and `\` and drive prefixes.

The imported function can be stored or passed as a callback with one or two string parameters, or wrapped in a closure:

```tsrust
import { basename } from "std:path";

function main() {
  const nameOf = (path: string): string => {
    return basename(path);
  };
  console.log(nameOf("/tmp/report.txt")); // report.txt
}
```

Ownership: arguments are borrowed; the result owns its contents.

examples.source:
import { basename } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(basename("/tmp/report.txt"));
    console.log(basename("/tmp/report.txt", ".txt"));
}

import { basename } from "std:path";
function main() {
    const nameOf = (path: string): string => { return basename(path); };
    console.log(nameOf("/tmp/report.txt"));
}

std:path.documentation:
Returns the lexical parent of a path without reading the filesystem or resolving dot components. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns independently owned text and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
import { dirname } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(dirname("/tmp/report.txt"));
} // output: /tmp
```

Empty input or a relative filename returns dot. Trailing separators are ignored. Original parent separators remain: dirname("a//b") returns "a/". POSIX dirname("//a") returns "//". Windows drive-relative names retain the drive prefix and UNC share roots remain intact. POSIX paths recognize slash; Windows paths recognize slash and backslash and drive-letter prefixes. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text, preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result owns its contents.

examples.source:
import { dirname } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(dirname("/tmp/report.txt"));
}

std:path.documentation:
Returns the suffix beginning at the final dot in the last path component without reading the filesystem. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns independently owned text and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
import { extname } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(extname("report.tar.gz"));
} // output: .gz
```

Trailing separators are ignored. Empty input, a leading-dot filename, dot and dot-dot return empty text. A final dot returns dot; extname("...") returns dot. Only the final suffix is returned. POSIX paths recognize slash; Windows paths recognize slash and backslash and drive-letter prefixes. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text, preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result owns its contents.

examples.source:
import { extname } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(extname("report.tar.gz"));
}

std:path.documentation:
Tests whether a path starts with platform root syntax without reading the filesystem. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns a boolean and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
import { isAbsolute } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(isAbsolute("/tmp/report.txt"));
} // output: true
```

Empty input is false. POSIX requires a leading slash. Windows accepts a leading slash or backslash, UNC syntax, and a drive letter followed by colon and a separator. C:report.txt is drive-relative and returns false. Dot components do not change root syntax. This test does not prove filesystem containment. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text. Unicode content is preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result is a boolean value.

Collapses repeated separators and resolves `.` and `..` components, without reading the filesystem. An empty path returns `.`. A trailing separator is kept; `..` cannot go above the root of an absolute path. Windows output uses `\` and keeps drive and UNC prefixes.

```tsrust
import { normalize } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(normalize("reports/../archive/"));
} // output: archive/
```

Symlinks are not resolved, so this is no containment check. Special Windows device names follow their own rules: `CON:` normalizes to `.\CON:.` and then `CON:.`.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result owns its contents.

Returns the path from `from` to `to`. Relative or empty inputs are resolved against the current directory first. Equal paths return "". Comparison is case-sensitive on POSIX and case-insensitive on Windows; a different Windows drive or server returns an absolute path. No trailing separator is kept.

```tsrust
import { relative } from "std:path";

function main() {
  console.log(relative("reports", "reports/2026"));
} // output: 2026
```

The paths need not exist and symlinks are not resolved. If the current directory is needed but cannot be read, throws an Error with code path-relative.

Ownership: both inputs are borrowed; the result owns its contents.

examples.source:
import { relative } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(relative("reports", "reports/2026"));
    console.log(relative("é😊", "é😊"));
}

std:path.documentation:
An independently owned lexical decomposition returned by parse. All five string fields are present, including empty fields. No path existence, normalization or symlink lookup is implied.

Ownership: owns every field; later input changes do not change this record.

```tsrust
import { parse } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const value = parse('file.tar.gz');
  console.log(value.root, value.dir, value.base, value.ext, value.name);
}
```

Expected output:   file.tar.gz .gz file.tar.

Optional string fields accepted by format. Missing and empty values have the same precedence. Fields are not coerced or normalized.

Ownership: this ordinary record owns supplied fields; format borrows it.

```tsrust
import { PathFormatOptions, format } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const options: PathFormatOptions = {
    name: 'notes', ext: 'txt'
  };
  console.log(format(options));
}
```

Expected output: notes.txt.

An owned recoverable resolve failure. Resolve returns this record in Err rather than throwing; unwrap may propagate it. Lexical absolute inputs avoid unnecessary directory observations.

Ownership: independently owns its message.

```tsrust
import { PathError } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const error: PathError = {
    code: 'Io', message: 'cwd unavailable'
  };
  console.log(error.code);
}
```

Expected output: Io.

Splits one required string using the executable target's path syntax. Returns root, dir, base, ext and name without changing spelling or normalizing dot segments. Empty input returns five empty strings. Dotfiles have no extension unless a later dot exists; trailing separators are excluded from base. No filesystem reads or recoverable errors occur.

Ownership: borrows path and returns independently owned fields.

```tsrust
import { parse } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const value = parse('file.tar.gz');
  console.log(value.root, value.dir, value.base, value.ext, value.name);
}
```

Expected output:   file.tar.gz .gz file.tar.

Composes an owned path from optional string fields. A nonempty dir overrides root; nonempty base overrides name plus ext. Missing and empty fields act alike. Add a dot for nonempty ext only when it has no leading dot. Insert the target separator between a non-root directory and the filename; preserve supplied spelling otherwise. All-empty options return an empty string. Does not normalize, check existence, coerce fields or mutate options.

Ownership: borrows the record and its fields; returns independent text.

```tsrust
import { PathFormatOptions, format } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const options: PathFormatOptions = {
    name: 'notes', ext: 'txt'
  };
  console.log(format(options));
}
```

Expected output: notes.txt.

Resolves zero or more borrowed string segments into an owned absolute lexical path. Empty segments are ignored; zero segments use the current directory. Scan from the right, stopping when an absolute root and any needed Windows device are known. Drive-relative paths use the corresponding drive directory when available, then the current directory. Dot segments and repeated separators are resolved lexically. Required OS context failures return Io; required non-Unicode context returns NotUnicode without lossy replacement. Fully qualified operands skip unneeded lookups. Does not canonicalize, inspect symlinks or establish containment.

Ownership: borrows every segment; Result owns either text or an error.

```tsrust
import { resolve } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const result = resolve();

  match (result) {
    Ok(value) => {
      console.log(value.length > 0);
    },
    Err(error) => {
      throw error.message;
    }
  }
}
```

Expected output: true.

Returns the executable target's directory separator: backslash on Windows, slash elsewhere. No arguments, mutation or recoverable errors.

Ownership: returns immutable text; no process state is borrowed.

```tsrust
import { separator } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const value = separator();
  console.log(value === '/' || value === '\\');
}
```

Expected output: true.

Returns the executable target's path-list delimiter: semicolon on Windows, colon elsewhere. No arguments, mutation or recoverable errors.

Ownership: returns immutable text; no process state is borrowed.

```tsrust
import { delimiter } from 'std:path';

function main() {
  const value = delimiter();
  console.log(value === ':' || value === ';');
}
```

Expected output: true.

Object.documentation:
Returns the property names of a value as a new string array. Record keys follow the order of the value's constructor literal.

@param value - A record gives its field names in the order of its object literal. An array or tuple gives "0" up to length minus one. A string gives its UTF-16 positions (a character outside the basic plane gives two). Numbers and booleans give an empty array.

Null, undefined, nullable values and functions are unsupported. Records have fixed fields: no added, deleted, inherited or symbol keys.

```tsrust
type Row = {
  first: int;
  second: string;
};
const row: Row = {
  second: "x", first: 1
};
console.log(Object.keys(row).join(",")); // prints "second,first"
```

```tsrust
console.log(Object.keys("é😀").join(","), Object.keys(true).length); // prints "0,1,2 0"
```

Ownership: The argument is borrowed. The returned array owns its entries independently of the argument.

Returns the field values of a record as a new array, in the same order as `Object.keys`. A tuple gives its positions in order; an array gives a copy of itself.

@returns An array such as `int[]` when every field has the same type; otherwise the element type is the union of the field types, such as `(int | string)[]`. Narrow an element with `typeof` or a null check before use. The value is evaluated once.

```tsrust
type ValuesSize = {
  w: int;
  h: int;
};
const size: ValuesSize = {
  h: 5, w: 4
};
const sides = Object.values(size);
console.log(sides, sides[0] + sides[1]); // prints "[ 5, 4 ] 9"
```

```tsrust
type ValuesRow = {
  id: int;
  name: string;
};
const row: ValuesRow = {
  id: 7, name: "ada"
};

for (const value of Object.values(row)) {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    console.log(value.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    console.log(value + 1);
  }
} // prints "8" then "ADA"
```

Strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, nullable values and records with a function field are `unsupported`.

Ownership: the argument is only read. Each value is copied, so the result is independent of the record; JavaScript shares nested arrays and objects instead.

examples.source:
type ValuesSize = { w: int; h: int; }; const size: ValuesSize = { h: 5, w: 4 };
const sides = Object.values(size);
console.log(sides, sides[0] + sides[1]);

type ValuesRow = { id: int; name: string; }; const row: ValuesRow = { id: 7, name: "ada" };
for (const value of Object.values(row)) {
    if (typeof value === "string") { console.log(value.toUpperCase()); } else { console.log(value + 1); }
}

Object.documentation:
Returns the fields of a record as a new array of `[name, value]` pairs, in the same order as `Object.keys`. A tuple gives pairs named `"0"`, `"1"` and so on. Read a pair with `pair[0]` and `pair[1]`; the value type is the field type, or the union of field types when they differ.

```tsrust
type EntriesRow = {
  id: int;
  name: string;
};
const row: EntriesRow = {
  name: "ada", id: 7
};

for (const pair of Object.entries(row)) {
  console.log(pair[0], String(pair[1]));
} // prints "name ada" then "id 7"
```

```tsrust
type EntriesSize = {
  w: int;
  h: int;
};
const size: EntriesSize = {
  w: 4, h: 5
};
console.log(Object.entries(size)); // prints "[ [ 'w', 4 ], [ 'h', 5 ] ]"
```

An array argument is `unsupported`; use `array.entries()` for `[index, value]` pairs with int positions. Strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, nullable values and records with a function field are also `unsupported`.

Ownership: the argument is only read. Each value is copied, so the result is independent of the record; JavaScript shares nested arrays and objects instead.

Checks whether a value has its own property named `key`. Inherited names such as toString do not count. A declared record field counts even when its value is undefined.

@param value - Record, tuple, array, string, number or boolean.
@param key - Property name; numbers, booleans, null and undefined are converted to text.

Arrays, tuples and strings own `length` and each index; string indexes count UTF-16 code units, so é😀 owns 0, 1 and 2. Names such as 01 and -0 are not indexes. Numbers and booleans own nothing.

```tsrust
import { strictEqual } from "std:testing";

function main() {
  const values = [4, 5];
  strictEqual(Object.hasOwn(values, "length"), true);
  strictEqual(Object.hasOwn(values, 2), false);
}
```

Ownership: borrows both arguments.

StringConstructor.documentation:
Builds a string from Unicode code points, one character per code, in argument order. Arrays and Sets of int codes can be spread in.

@param codes - Int code points from 0 through 1114111 (0x10FFFF). Spread an `int[]` or `Set<int>` to pass many: `String.fromCodePoint(...codes)`. A `number` is a `type` error; convert first.

@returns A new string; no arguments gives `""`.

```tsrust
const codes = [116, 115, 114];
console.log(String.fromCodePoint(65, 233, 128512), String.fromCodePoint(...codes)); // prints "Aé😀 tsr"
```

A code below 0 or above 1114111 stops the program with `String.fromCodePoint code point must be between 0 and 1114111` and the source position of the call.

Codes 55296 through 57343 (0xD800 to 0xDFFF) are surrogate halves. A high half followed by its low half forms one character; a half with no partner becomes U+FFFD (the replacement character), since strings always hold valid Unicode.

```tsrust
console.log(String.fromCodePoint(...new Set<int>([65, 128578])));
// prints A🙂
```

Ownership: the codes are only read; the result is a new string.

Builds a string from UTF-16 code units, in argument order. Use it for codes up to 65535; use `String.fromCodePoint` for full Unicode code points. Arrays and Sets of int codes can be spread in.

@param codes - Int code units; only the low 16 bits are used, so 65601 gives the same character as 65. Spread an `int[]` or `Set<int>` to pass many. A `number` is a `type` error.

@returns A new string; no arguments gives `""`.

```tsrust
const codes = [72, 105];
console.log(String.fromCharCode(...codes), String.fromCharCode(55357, 56832)); // prints "Hi 😀"
```

A high surrogate (55296 to 56319) followed by a low surrogate (56320 to 57343) forms one character. A surrogate with no partner becomes U+FFFD (the replacement character), since strings always hold valid Unicode. No value causes an error.

```tsrust
console.log(String.fromCharCode(...new Set<int>([65, 66])));
// prints AB
```

Ownership: the codes are only read; the result is a new string.

Joins text segments with values in between, without decoding backslash escapes in the segments. Extra substitutions are ignored and missing substitutions are empty.

@param template - A record with a `raw: string[]` field. An empty array returns `""`; one segment returns that segment.
@param substitutions - Values inserted between consecutive segments, converted with `String(value)`. Extra values are evaluated but not used; missing ones are empty. Spread a string array or Set to insert several.

```tsrust
type Template = {
  raw: string[];
};
const template: Template = {
  raw: ["Hello ", "!"]
};
console.log(String.raw(template, "Ada")); // prints Hello Ada!
```

```tsrust
type Template = {
  raw: string[];
};
const template: Template = {
  raw: ["a", "b", "c"]
};
const values = ["X", "Y"];
console.log(String.raw(template, ...values), String.raw(template, 7));
// prints aXbYc a7bc
```

Tagged-template syntax and a plain string as `raw` are unsupported.

Ownership: borrows the template and substitutions; returns a new string.

```tsrust
const inserts = new Set<string>(["x", "y"]);
console.log(String.raw({
  raw: ["<", ":", ">"]
}, ...inserts), inserts.size);
// prints <x:y> 2
```

Tuple.documentation:
Returns the number of declared positions in a fixed tuple. Takes no arguments. The result is int; the tuple is unchanged and length cannot be assigned. An empty tuple has length zero. Each position keeps its declared type and is accessed with a constant integer index. Out-of-range indices are rejected. Tuple positions may be replaced with values of their declared types; variable-length array mutation methods are unavailable.

```tsrust
const pair: [int, string] = [2, "cat"];
console.log(pair.length, pair[0], pair[1]);
// 2 2 cat
const empty: [] = [];
console.log(empty.length);
// 0
```

Ownership: length borrows the tuple. Owned positional values transfer independently. Restore a transferred position before observing the whole tuple; use clone() to retain an independent copy.

std:testing-runtime.documentation:
Returns void when condition is true. Otherwise stops the current test body; outside a running test it stops native execution with AssertionError followed by message. Requires one boolean and one string; wrong arity or types are compile-time errors. No receiver changes.

```tsrust
import { ok } from "std:testing";

function main() {
  ok(true, "check");
  console.log("passed");
}
// passed
```

Ownership: condition is copied and message is borrowed.

Expectation.documentation:
Fails unless the retained actual value and expected compare equal with Object.is semantics. Takes one expected value of the same concrete type. Returns void on success and leaves the assertion object reusable. Number NaN equals itself; positive and negative zero differ. Strings compare exact text; arrays and records compare identity. Integers retain signed 64-bit values. Data unions compare their active alternatives; callable values are unsupported. Wrong arity or incompatible types are compile-time errors. Failure stops the current test body; outside a running test it stops native execution with AssertionError.

@param expected - Expected value with the same concrete type as actual.

```tsrust
import { expect } from "std:testing";

function main() {
  expect(Number.NaN).toBe(Number.NaN);
  console.log("passed");
}
// passed
```

Ownership: the assertion object and expected values are borrowed. The assertion object owns its captured actual value.

Checks that the retained actual value is truthy after Boolean conversion. Takes no arguments and returns void on success. False, numeric zero including negative zero, NaN, empty strings, null and undefined are falsy; other supported values, including empty arrays and records, are truthy. Failure stops the current test body; outside a running test it stops native execution with AssertionError. Extra arguments are compile-time errors. The assertion object remains reusable and no receiver is changed. Unsupported value types remain subject to the generic expectation's type rules.

```tsrust
import { expect } from "std:testing";

function main() {
  expect("é").toBeTruthy();
  console.log("passed");
}
// passed
```

