std:fs.documentation:
Opens an existing path read-only at cursor zero. Does not create or truncate files. Returns Ok(File) or an open error; missing paths, permissions and embedded NUL are reported. Opening a directory may succeed, but reading it fails. Paths follow normal filesystem symlink resolution. The example assumes input.bin exists. Ownership: path is borrowed; success creates a unique file owner.

File operations use the host filesystem. On Windows, file release cannot report OS close errors; buffered flush errors remain observable.

```tsrust
import { open, close } from "std:fs";

function main() {
  match (open("input.bin")) {
    Ok(file) => {
      match (close(file)) {
        Ok(_) => {
          console.log("closed");
        },
        Err(error) => {
          throw error.message;
        }
      }
    },
    Err(error) => {
      throw error.message;
    }
  }
}
```

Expected output: closed.

Reads up to `length` bytes from the current position into new `Bytes`. A shorter result is normal, and an empty result means end of file; it does not read to the end. `length` 0 returns empty bytes without reading. A negative `length` returns `length-range`. No text decoding is done.

A `BufferedReader` returns at most its buffered bytes or one refill per call.

```tsrust
import { length } from "std:bytes";
import { open, read, close } from "std:fs";

function main() {
  match (open("input.bin")) {
    Ok(file) => {
