Replaces a text file atomically: writes `text` as UTF-8 to a temporary file in the same directory, syncs it, renames it over `path` and syncs the directory. Readers see either the old or the new contents. The parent directory must exist. No BOM or newline is added.

@param expectedText - Optional: the exact current contents. If the file is missing or differs, returns `conflict` and changes nothing. This is not a lock; an uncooperative writer can still race the final rename.

New files are private (mode 0600, subject to umask). An existing target must be a readable, writable regular file with one hard link; symlinks, directories and read-only files return an error unchanged. macOS keeps the existing owner, permissions, ACLs and extended attributes; other Unix systems keep permissions only. Windows currently returns unsupported.

On most errors the file is unchanged. `committed-not-durable` means the new contents are in place but the directory sync failed: keep a recovery copy, and do not simply retry with the old `expectedText` (it will conflict). Open readers keep the old file.

Ownership: borrows the arguments.

```tsrust
import { writeTextFileAtomic, readTextFile, removeFile } from 'std:fs';
import { platform } from 'std:os';

function main() {
