import { fromHex, readFloat32 } from "std:bytes";

function main() {
  const bytes = unwrap(fromHex("0000c03f"));
  console.log(unwrap(readFloat32(bytes, 0, "little")));
}

std:bytes.documentation:
Writes an IEEE-754 binary32 value into 4 bytes, allowing unaligned offsets. The number is rounded to binary32 using round-to-nearest with ties to even; finite overflow produces signed infinity. Conversion is not restricted to exactly representable values. NaN, infinities and signed zero are accepted; NaN payload identity is not promised. A negative offset, overflow or incomplete destination returns range with the requested offset before any write. Every failure leaves all bytes unchanged. No allocation or resizing occurs. Every argument is required; there are no omitted-argument defaults.

```tsrust
import { alloc, writeFloat32, toHex } from "std:bytes";

function main() {
  const bytes = unwrap(alloc(4));
  unwrap(writeFloat32(bytes, 0, 1.5, "big"));
  console.log(unwrap(toHex(bytes)));
}
```

Expected output: 3fc00000.

Ownership: borrows the destination exclusively for this call. A ByteViewMut writes through to its owner. The result carries no source loan.

