OrderedSet.documentation:
The number of distinct stored keys, including at most one NaN and one zero key. This read-only property takes no arguments, returns a nonnegative int, and does not change the set. Ownership: reading size borrows the receiver.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  console.log(values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 1.

Insert value if no equal key is present. value must have the declared element type. A duplicate leaves size unchanged; signed zeros and NaNs follow the set equality rule. Returns void and may change the set. Ownership: an owned argument transfers even when it duplicates an existing key.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  values.add(2);
  console.log(values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 1.

Test whether an equal key is stored. value must have the declared element type; an empty set returns false. Returns boolean without changing the set. Lookup follows the key order. Ownership: the receiver and argument are borrowed; string queries remain reusable and are not copied.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  console.log(values.has(2), values.has(9));
}
```

Expected output: true false.

Remove the equal key if present. value must have the declared element type. Returns true after removal, or false when no key matched, including on an empty set. Ownership: the receiver is exclusively borrowed; the query argument is borrowed and remains reusable.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  console.log(values.delete(2), values.delete(2), values.size);
}
```

Expected output: true false 0.

Remove all keys. Takes no arguments, returns void and leaves size zero. Clearing an empty set succeeds. Ownership: exclusively borrows the receiver and releases its stored keys.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  values.clear();
  console.log(values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 0.

Returns an independent array of all unique elements in ascending key order. Takes no arguments and leaves the set unchanged. Empty sets return an empty array. Numeric order places NaN after infinity, treats all NaNs equally and normalizes signed zeros to positive zero; strings use Unicode scalar lexicographic order without locale collation.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<string>();
  values.add("b");
  values.add("a");
  console.log(values.values().join(","));
}
```

Expected output: a,b.

Ownership: Borrows the receiver during the call; clones elements into owned array storage.

Returns an independent array of all unique elements in ascending key order. Takes no arguments and leaves the set unchanged. Empty sets return an empty array. Numeric order places NaN after infinity, treats all NaNs equally and normalizes signed zeros to positive zero; strings use Unicode scalar lexicographic order without locale collation.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<string>();
  values.add("b");
  values.add("a");
  console.log(values.keys().join(","));
}
```

Expected output: a,b.

Ownership: Borrows the receiver during the call; clones elements into owned array storage.

Returns independent [value, value] tuples in ascending key order. Takes no arguments, leaves the set unchanged and returns an empty array when empty. Both tuple positions contain the same value. Numeric keys retain the set order: canonical positive zero, equal NaNs after infinity.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  values.add(1);
  const rows = values.entries();
  console.log(rows[0][0], rows[0][1], rows.length);
}
```

Expected output: 1 1 2.

Ownership: Borrows the set and clones each key into both tuple positions.

Calls callback once per element in ascending key order, supplying the value twice and then the source set. The single callback may omit trailing parameters. Returns void, ignores callback results and makes no calls for an empty set. Callback errors propagate. The source cannot be mutated or transferred during traversal. Numeric order keeps NaNs after infinity and signed zeros canonical.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<int>();
  values.add(2);
  values.add(1);
  let text = "";
  values.forEach((value, key) => {
    text += String(value) + String(key);
  });
  console.log(text);
}
```

Expected output: 1122.

Ownership: Borrows the set and owned element arguments for each callback invocation; no snapshot is created.

Reads the least stored key, or undefined when empty. Takes no arguments and leaves the receiver unchanged. Keys use ascending total order: numbers compare numerically with NaNs equal and after infinity, signed zeros equal and stored as positive zero; strings compare lexicographically by Unicode scalar value.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<string>();
  values.add("b");
  values.add("a");
  console.log(values.first()!);
}
```

Expected output: a.

Ownership: Scalar keys copy. Owned string keys borrow readonly from a stored receiver; mutation or transfer is rejected while the borrow remains in scope. Temporary receivers cannot provide owned endpoint borrows.

Reads the greatest stored key, or undefined when empty. Takes no arguments and leaves the receiver unchanged. Keys use ascending total order: numbers compare numerically with NaNs equal and after infinity, signed zeros equal and stored as positive zero; strings compare lexicographically by Unicode scalar value.

```tsrust
import { OrderedSet } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedSet<string>();
  values.add("b");
  values.add("a");
  console.log(values.last()!);
}
```

Expected output: b.

Ownership: Scalar keys copy. Owned string keys borrow readonly from a stored receiver; mutation or transfer is rejected while the borrow remains in scope. Temporary receivers cannot provide owned endpoint borrows.

Heap.documentation:
Returns the readonly number of stored values. Takes no arguments and leaves the heap unchanged. Invalid argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
import { Heap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new Heap<int>();
  values.push(3);
  console.log(values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 1.

Ownership: reads the heap; returns a copied int.

Transfers one value of the declared element type into the heap. Returns void and increases size by one. Equal values are stored separately. Invalid argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
import { Heap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new Heap<int>();
  values.push(3);
  values.push(9);
  console.log(values.pop()!);
}
```

Expected output: 9.

Ownership: requires mutable access to the heap and takes ownership of value.

Returns the greatest value without removal, or undefined when empty. Takes no arguments. Scalar results copy; owned string results borrow from a stored receiver. Mutation is rejected while that borrow remains live. Invalid argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
import { Heap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new Heap<int>();
  values.push(3);
  console.log(values.peek(), values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 3 1.

Ownership: reads the heap; an owned element is borrowed while the result is in use.

Removes and returns the greatest value, or undefined when empty. Takes no arguments. Removal transfers an owned value to the caller. Invalid argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
import { Heap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new Heap<int>();
  values.push(3);
  console.log(values.pop(), values.size, values.pop());
}
```

Expected output: 3 0 undefined.

Ownership: requires mutable access to the heap; the removed element is transferred to the caller.

Releases every stored value and sets size to zero. Takes no arguments and returns void. Invalid argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
import { Heap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new Heap<int>();
  values.push(3);
  values.clear();
  console.log(values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 0.

Ownership: requires mutable access to the heap; removed elements are released.

Returns an independent array sorted from least to greatest. Takes no arguments, preserves the heap and returns an empty array when empty. Numeric keys compare numerically with NaNs equal and after infinity, and signed zeros normalized to positive zero. Strings use Unicode scalar lexicographic order; no locale collation occurs. This array order is ascending even though pop removes the greatest key.

```tsrust
import { Heap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new Heap<int>();
  values.push(3);
  values.push(1);
  values.push(2);
  console.log(values.toSortedArray().join(","), values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 1,2,3 3.

Ownership: Borrows the receiver during the call and clones keys into independently owned sorted storage.

OrderedMap.documentation:
Returns the number of distinct keys as int. Readonly property with no arguments; assignment is rejected. Empty maps have size 0. The receiver is unchanged.

Keys must match the declared int, number, boolean, string or enum type; mixed unions and object identity keys are unsupported. Numbers use numeric order, with signed zeros equal and NaNs equal and greater than infinity. Strings use Unicode scalar lexicographic order without locale collation or normalization. Booleans place false before true; enums use their backing values. Wrong argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
import { OrderedMap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedMap<string, int>();
  values.set("b", 2);
  values.set("a", 1);
  console.log(values.size);
}
```

Expected output: 2

Ownership: Reading size borrows the receiver.

Adds `key` with `value`, or replaces the value of an equal key (the size stays the same). Entries stay in key order.

Keys must be `int`, `number`, `boolean`, `string` or enum values of the declared type. Numbers sort numerically (`-0` equals `0`; NaN sorts after infinity), strings by Unicode code point (no locale rules), `false` before `true`, enums by their values.

```tsrust
import { OrderedMap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedMap<string, int>();
  values.set("b", 2);
  values.set("a", 1);
  values.set("a", 3);
  console.log(values.size, values.get("a")!);
}
```

Expected output: 2 3

Ownership: moves the key and value into the map; a replaced value is dropped.

Returns the value for `key`, or `undefined` if there is none. Nothing is inserted.

```tsrust
import { OrderedMap } from "std:collections";

function main() {
  const values = new OrderedMap<string, int>();
  values.set("b", 2);
  values.set("a", 1);
  console.log(values.get("a") ?? 0, values.get("missing") ?? 0);
}
```

Expected output: 1 0

Ownership: borrows the key. Numbers and booleans are copied; other values are borrowed from the map.

Returns true when key is present and false otherwise. Takes one key argument and leaves the receiver unchanged.

Keys must match the declared int, number, boolean, string or enum type; mixed unions and object identity keys are unsupported. Numbers use numeric order, with signed zeros equal and NaNs equal and greater than infinity. Strings use Unicode scalar lexicographic order without locale collation or normalization. Booleans place false before true; enums use their backing values. Wrong argument counts and incompatible types are static errors.

```tsrust
