std:env.documentation:
Describes an environment or process-argument failure. code is InvalidName for an empty name or a name containing = or NUL, or NotUnicode when a value or process argument cannot be represented as Unicode. message explains the failure without including the environment value or argument. Missing variables are successful lookups, not errors. Fields are ordinary strings.

```tsrust
import { get } from "std:env";

function main() {
  match (get("BAD=NAME")) {
    Ok(value) => {
      console.log(value ?? "missing");
    },
    Err(error) => {
      console.log(error.code);
    }
  }
}
// prints: InvalidName
```

Io reports an OS path observation failure. Non-Unicode path and snapshot failures use NotUnicode, with no rejected value in the message.

Ownership: an owned record; explicit clone creates independent storage.

Reads a variable from the running process environment. Returns Ok(value) with an independent UTF-8 string when present, Ok(undefined) when missing, or Err(EnvError). An empty value returns Ok(""). Empty names and names containing = or NUL fail with InvalidName. Non-Unicode values fail with NotUnicode; no lossy replacement occurs. Lookup uses host name matching rules and does not mutate the environment. Values are read at execution time.

```tsrust
import { get } from "std:env";

function main() {
  match (get("A=B")) {
    Ok(_) => {
      throw "unexpected success";
    },
    Err(error) => {
      console.log(error.code);
    }
  }
}
```

Expected output: InvalidName. Ownership: borrows name without consuming it; successful strings own their storage.

Returns the program's arguments, without the executable name, as a new array in order. Empty strings, spaces and Unicode are kept; flags are not parsed. An argument that is not valid Unicode returns `Err` with code NotUnicode (without revealing it).

Use `tsrust run main.tsr -- first "two words"` to forward arguments. Unlike Node process.argv, this result contains neither a runtime path nor a script path.

```tsrust
import { args } from "std:env";

function main() {
  const values = unwrap(args());
  console.log(values.length);
}
```

Expected output when launched without arguments: 0.

Ownership: each call returns a new array.

One owned environment name/value pair returned by vars. Empty values remain empty; names are not filtered through get's lookup-name validator. Snapshot ordering is unspecified.

Ownership: both strings belong to this record and remain independent of process state.

```tsrust
import { EnvVar } from 'std:env';

function main() {
  const row: EnvVar = {
    name: 'APP_MODE', value: ''
  };
  console.log(row.name, row.value.length);
}
```

Expected output: APP_MODE 0.

Reads a complete owned snapshot of process environment pairs with no arguments. Preserves empty values and OS-provided names, including names that get cannot look up. Ordering is unspecified and later process changes do not alter the snapshot. If any name or value is not Unicode, returns NotUnicode with no partial array or lossy replacement. Error text does not disclose the rejected value. Does not mutate process state.

Ownership: Result owns the array and every pair.

```tsrust
import { vars } from 'std:env';

function main() {
  match (vars()) {
    Ok(rows) => {
      for (const row of rows) {
        if (typeof row.name !== 'string' || typeof row.value !== 'string') {
          throw 'invalid snapshot';
        }
      }
    },
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'NotUnicode') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('snapshot observed');
}
```

Expected output: snapshot observed.

Returns the current working directory as owned Unicode text. No arguments. OS lookup failures return Io; non-Unicode paths return NotUnicode. Does not change directories or add normalization.

Ownership: success owns its text; absence borrows no state.

```tsrust
import { currentDir } from 'std:env';

function main() {
  match (currentDir()) {
    Ok(value) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'Io' && error.code !== 'NotUnicode') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('directory observed');
}
```

Expected output: directory observed.

Returns the running executable path as owned Unicode text. No arguments. OS lookup failures return Io; non-Unicode paths return NotUnicode. Does not resolve additional symlinks, strip OS decorations or promise that the file still exists.

Ownership: success owns its text; absence borrows no state.

```tsrust
import { currentExe } from 'std:env';

function main() {
  match (currentExe()) {
    Ok(value) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'Io' && error.code !== 'NotUnicode') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('executable observed');
}
```

Expected output: executable observed.

Returns the OS-selected temporary-directory path as owned Unicode text. No arguments. Uses platform environment and OS fallback rules, preserving any returned empty path. Non-Unicode paths return NotUnicode. Creates nothing and does not promise that the path exists or is private.

Ownership: success owns its text; absence borrows no state.

```tsrust
import { tempDir } from 'std:env';

function main() {
  match (tempDir()) {
    Ok(value) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'Io' && error.code !== 'NotUnicode') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('temporary directory observed');
}
```

Expected output: temporary directory observed.

Returns the current user home directory when known, or Ok(undefined) when unavailable or unsupported. No arguments. Uses the platform environment and account-directory fallback rules. A returned empty path remains an empty string; non-Unicode paths return NotUnicode. Does not create or validate the directory.

Ownership: success owns its text; absence borrows no state.

```tsrust
import { homeDir } from 'std:env';

function main() {
  match (homeDir()) {
    Ok(value) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      if (error.code !== 'Io' && error.code !== 'NotUnicode') {
        throw error.message;
      }
    }
  }

  console.log('home directory observed');
}
```

Expected output: home directory observed.

std:time.documentation:
Measures elapsed time using a process-local monotonic clock. Takes no arguments and has no defaults or argument errors. Returns a nonnegative number of milliseconds, including fractional milliseconds, relative to an origin established by the first call. Subtract two readings from the same process to measure a duration; readings are not calendar timestamps and must not be compared across processes. Consecutive readings may be equal because clock resolution varies. System-clock adjustments do not change the origin. Whether suspended time counts is platform dependent, and floating-point precision decreases over long runtimes. No receiver is mutated.

```tsrust
