std:schema.documentation:
Opaque schema with inferred output type T. Construct through the imported s builders. A Schema<string> cannot be assigned to Schema<int>. It cannot be constructed as a record, inspected as data or implicitly copied. Scalar safeParse returns a typed Result.

```tsrust
import { s } from 'std:schema';

function main() {
  const schema = s.string();
  const result = schema.safeParse(JSON.parse('42'));

  if (result.success) {
    const text = result.data;
    console.log(text);
  } else {
    const error = result.error;
    console.log(error.issues.length, error.truncated);
  }
}
```

Expected output:
```text
1 false
```

Runtime input validation failure returned by safeParse. Contains issues and a truncated flag indicating omitted issues. Each issue includes a code, property/index path and controlled type summaries; input values are never retained. Invalid rule definitions instead throw SchemaConstructionError. Ownership: the error owns its issue collection.

```tsrust
import { s } from 'std:schema';

function main() {
  const schema = s.string().min(1);
  console.log(schema.parse('Hello'));

  try {
    schema.parse('');
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.issues.length);
  }
}
```

Expected output:
```text
Hello
1
```

One validation issue: code, path, pathTruncated, expected and observed. The root path is empty. expected and observed describe types rather than input contents. Ownership: owns its strings and path.

```tsrust
import { s } from 'std:schema';

function main() {
  const schema = s.object({
    name: s.string()
  });
  const result = schema.safeParse(JSON.parse('{"name":3}'));

  if (result.success) {
    const value = result.data;
    throw 'Expected rejection';
  } else {
    const error = result.error;
    const issue = error.issues[0];
    console.log(issue.path.length);
  }
}
```

Expected output:
```text
1
```

One path segment. A field segment has field set and index absent; an array segment has index set and field absent. Indices are zero-based. Root issues have no segments. Ownership: owns any field name.

```tsrust
import { s } from "std:schema";

function main() {
  const schema = s.object({
    items: s.array(s.object({
      qty: s.int()
    }))
  });
  const result = schema.safeParse(JSON.parse('{"items":[{"qty":1},{"qty":"two"}]}'));

  if (!result.success) {
    for (const segment of result.error.issues[0].path) {
      console.log(segment.field ?? "-", segment.index ?? -1);
    }
  }
}
// prints: items -1
// prints: - 1
// prints: qty -1
```

Thrown when a schema rule cannot be represented by JSON Schema export. Fields: code is unrepresentable; path is a JSON Pointer into the schema rule (empty for the root); reason explains the unsupported rule. Pointer segments escape ~ as ~0 and / as ~1. Export does not execute callbacks or default factories and never returns a partial document. Ownership: the error owns its path; catching it does not consume the source schema.

```tsrust
import { s } from 'std:schema';

function main() {
  const schema = s.string().refine(value => value.length > 0);

  try {
    schema.toJsonSchema();
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.code);
  }
}
```

Expected output:
```text
unrepresentable
```

Thrown when constructing invalid schema bounds. code is negative-length for string limits, nonfinite-limit for floating limits, or contradictory-bounds; operation identifies min, max, gt or lt; limit retains the supplied int or number without converting exact integers to floating point. Narrow the limit before arithmetic. This error concerns schema construction, not input validation through safeParse. Contains no input data.

