ThreadPool.documentation:
Submits an owned callback with zero arguments or one TaskContext to this pool. T is its return type and E its inferred thrown error. Starts when a worker is available. Admission returns full when outstanding capacity is exhausted or closed after shutdown; it never waits for capacity. Completion frees the worker, but retains a capacity slot until join or handle cleanup; detached work releases its slot on completion. Changes this pool only.

Ownership: borrows the ThreadPool and consumes callback captures. Caller loans and program state cannot cross workers; the returned handle owns its result.

```tsrust
import { join, ThreadPool, Thread } from 'std:threads';

function calculate(jobs: ReadOnly<ThreadPool>): Thread<int, undefined> {
  return unwrap(jobs.task((): int => 42));
}

function main() {
  const pool = new ThreadPool({
    workers: 2, capacity: 16
  });
  const shared = unwrap(pool.task((): int => 7));
  {
    const jobs = new ThreadPool({
      workers: 1, capacity: 4
    });
    const background = new ThreadPool({
      workers: 2, capacity: 8
    });
    const answer = calculate(jobs);
    const other = unwrap(background.task((): string => 'independent'));
    unwrap(jobs.shutdown());
    console.log(unwrap(join(answer)));
    console.log(unwrap(join(other)));
    console.log(jobs.stats().outstanding);
  }

  console.log(unwrap(join(shared)));
  unwrap(pool.shutdown());
}
// 42
// independent
// 0
// 7
```
Expected output: 42, independent, 0, 7 on separate lines.

