ThreadPool.documentation:
Takes no arguments. Returns a new snapshot of this pool: workers, capacity, queued, running, completed and outstanding. outstanding equals queued + running + completed; completed counts retained uncollected results. Does not change the pool; concurrent work can change counts immediately. Remains usable after shutdown.

Ownership: borrows the pool and returns an owned record.

```tsrust
import { ThreadPool } from "std:threads";

function main() {
  const pool = new ThreadPool({
    workers: 1, capacity: 4
  });
  const a = unwrap(pool.task((): int => 1));
  const b = unwrap(pool.task((): int => 2));
  console.log(pool.stats().outstanding); // both tasks hold a slot until joined
  console.log(unwrap(a.join()) + unwrap(b.join()));
  console.log(pool.stats().outstanding);
}
// prints: 2
// prints: 3
// prints: 0
```
Expected output: 42, independent, 0, 7 on separate lines.

