ThreadPool.documentation:
Takes no arguments. Permanently closes this instance; it cannot restart. Create a fresh ThreadPool for new work. Closes this pool to new tasks, then waits for queued and running work, including detached tasks. Does not cancel work. Repeated calls succeed. Other pools and dedicated threads are unaffected. Retained task handles can still be joined. Scope exit performs the same drain automatically. May wait indefinitely for nonterminating callbacks.

Ownership: borrows the pool; does not consume task handles or their results.

```tsrust
import { ThreadPool } from "std:threads";

function main() {
  const pool = new ThreadPool({
    workers: 2, capacity: 4
  });
  const job = unwrap(pool.task((): int => 7));
  unwrap(pool.shutdown()); // waits for queued and running work
  console.log(unwrap(job.join())); // retained handles can still be joined
  match (pool.task((): int => 8)) {
    Ok(_) => {},
    Err(error) => {
      console.log(error.code);
    }
  }
}
// prints: 7
// prints: closed
```
Expected output: 42, independent, 0, 7 on separate lines.

