std:http.documentation:
Creates an Axum-backed router with Hono-style middleware and request contexts. State is stored once and exposed read-only as c.env to every handler. Variables declares the request-local keys accepted by c.set and c.get. Both type arguments default to null. Register callbacks in execution order; listen consumes the router and returns Server.

```tsrust
type State = {
  greeting: string;
};
const app = new Router<State>({
  greeting: "Hello"
});
app.get("/", c => c.text(clone(c.env.greeting)));
```

GET / returns Hello. Mutable access to application state, caller loans, local caches and task handles are rejected; use supported concurrent handles for shared operations. Ownership: consumes state once and releases it after the server and request work finish.

A request-local context supplied by Router. req exposes the incoming request. header changes outgoing headers before or after downstream middleware; text, html and json construct responses. Context has no public constructor or clone operation.

```tsrust
function hello(c: Mutable<Context>): Response {
  return c.text('Hello');
}
```

Returns a UTF-8 response with status 200. Ownership: handlers exclusively borrow Context for the invocation; it cannot be retained in detached work.

State and Variables match the supplying Router. env exposes shared application state. set/get store checked request-local values, initially absent for each new request.

res.documentation:
Read-only current response. After await next(), inspect c.res.status() or c.res.header(name). Before downstream returns a response, status is 404. Use c.header to change response headers. The response and body cannot be moved out of Context.

```tsrust
await next();
console.log(c.res.status());
```

Prints 200 when the downstream handler returns c.text("ok"). Reading does not consume the response.

std:http.documentation:
Runs the remaining middleware and route handler, then resumes the current middleware. Returns a lazy `Task<void>`, not a `Response`; read the result through `c.res` after awaiting. Await it before touching the context again; `next` runs downstream handlers and cannot be cloned.

```tsrust
app.use(async (c, next) => {
  c.header('x-before', 'entry');
  await next();
  c.header('x-after', 'exit');
});
```

Returning a `Response` without calling `next()` skips the rest of the chain. Finishing with neither returns 500. Failures downstream become a 500 response so outer middleware still finishes.

Ownership: `next()` can be called once and borrows the `Context` until awaited.

An owned HTTP request. method(), url(), param(name) and header(name) inspect metadata. setHeader changes metadata. text() and body() consume the request; a body can be read once. Body reads enforce a cumulative 1048576-byte limit. Each handler receives a distinct request.

Ownership: unique, nonclonable capability; consuming operations transfer ownership.

```tsrust
import { Request } from 'std:http';

function inspect(value: Request): void {}

function main() {
  console.log('ok');
}
```
Expected output: ok.

An owned HTTP response. status() and header(name) inspect metadata; setHeader changes metadata before transmission. text() or body() consumes the response. Client responses retain HTTP error statuses as data. Dropping the body releases its transport resources.

Ownership: unique, nonclonable capability; consuming operations transfer ownership.

```tsrust
import { Response } from 'std:http';

function inspect(value: Response): void {}

function main() {
  console.log('ok');
}
```
Expected output: ok.

A single-owner HTTP byte stream. Await next() for Bytes or undefined at end; await text() to consume strict UTF-8. Reads apply backpressure and enforce a cumulative 1048576-byte limit. Dropping an unread body releases its resources.

Ownership: unique, nonclonable capability; consuming operations transfer ownership.

```tsrust
import { Body } from 'std:http';

function inspect(value: Body): void {}

function main() {
  console.log('ok');
}
```
Expected output: ok.

An owned running HTTP listener. address() returns its IP and port. Await shutdown() to stop accepting and drain requests, using an application drain deadline only when shutdownTimeoutMs was explicitly configured; otherwise graceful shutdown can remain pending for stalled I/O. Dropping the handle requests cleanup; its async owner drains completion.

Ownership: unique, nonclonable capability; consuming operations transfer ownership.

```tsrust
import { Server } from 'std:http';

function inspect(value: Server): void {}

function main() {
  console.log('ok');
}
```
Expected output: ok.

An owned middleware continuation. Await run(request) to invoke the remaining middleware and handler once. It consumes both the continuation and request. Returning without running it short-circuits the chain.

Ownership: unique, nonclonable capability; consuming operations transfer ownership.

```tsrust
import { Next } from 'std:http';

function inspect(value: Next): void {}

function main() {
  console.log('ok');
}
```
Expected output: ok.

A checked HTTP failure with code and message strings. Codes describe invalid method, route, address, status, header, URL, body, encoding, limit, listener, client, request or timeout. Catch at the failing operation; an uncaught handler failure becomes a generic 500 without exposing this message.

Ownership: ordinary owned error data.

```tsrust
import { HttpError } from 'std:http';

function inspect(value: HttpError): void {}

function main() {
  console.log('ok');
}
```
Expected output: ok.

HTTPS listener credentials. Both PEM strings are required: cert is the certificate chain, leaf first; key is its matching unencrypted private key. Invalid or mismatched credentials throw HttpError code tls when listen is awaited. No client certificate authentication. The listener keeps independent settings after startup.

Ownership: owned record; listen consumes it when creating the task.

```tsrust
import { TlsOptions } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const options: TlsOptions = {
    cert: "PEM", key: "PEM"
  };
  console.log(options.cert);
}
```
Expected output: PEM.

Options for `new Client(...)`.

Optional settings:
- `ca`: a PEM CA bundle that replaces the public roots for this client; certificate and hostname checks stay on. Invalid PEM throws `HttpError` code `tls`.
- `bodyLimit`: maximum bytes per response body (no limit by default); exceeding it throws code `limit`.
- `timeoutMs`, `connectTimeoutMs`: deadlines in milliseconds (none by default).
- `followRedirects`, `maxRedirects`: redirects are followed up to 10 times by default; `followRedirects: false` returns them instead. The two options conflict.
- `proxy`, `noProxy`: set an explicit proxy URL or disable proxies (they conflict); by default the system proxy settings are used.

Invalid values or conflicts throw `HttpError` code `options`. A `bodyLimit` of 0 allows only empty bodies. Streamed request bodies cannot be resent on a redirect.

Ownership: the client copies these settings.

```tsrust
import { Client } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const client = new Client({
    bodyLimit: 4096
  });
  console.log("bounded client");
}
```

timeoutMs.documentation:
Optional nonnegative total request deadline in milliseconds, covering connect through complete response body. Zero installs an immediate deadline. Omitted or undefined installs no total request/body timeout. Must fit the backend Instant range; invalid values throw HttpError code options. Expiry before headers throws timeout; expiry while reading the returned body throws body. Settings are copied during construction.

Ownership: borrowed at construction; settings are retained independently.

```tsrust
import { ClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const options: ClientOptions = {
    timeoutMs: 1000
  };
  console.log(options.timeoutMs);
}
```

connectTimeoutMs.documentation:
Optional nonnegative connection-phase timeout in milliseconds. Zero installs an immediate connect deadline. Omitted or undefined preserves the backend default of no separate connect timeout. Must fit the backend Instant range; invalid values throw HttpError code options. A total timeout can expire first. Settings are copied during construction.

Ownership: borrowed at construction; settings are retained independently.

```tsrust
import { ClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const options: ClientOptions = {
    connectTimeoutMs: 250
  };
  console.log(options.connectTimeoutMs);
}
```

followRedirects.documentation:
Optional redirect policy. False returns redirect responses without following them; true or omission uses backend following, with a default maximum of10 redirects unless maxRedirects is supplied. False conflicts with any supplied maxRedirects and throws HttpError code options. Cross-origin redirects strip sensitive authorization/cookie headers. Buffered request bodies can replay; streaming bodies are not collected to make redirects replayable. Copied during construction.

Ownership: borrowed at construction; settings are retained independently.

```tsrust
import { ClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const options: ClientOptions = {
    followRedirects: false
  };
  console.log(options.followRedirects);
}
```

maxRedirects.documentation:
Optional nonnegative, target-representable redirect count. Supplying it enables backend following up to that limit. Zero rejects the first redirect with HttpError code request; it differs from followRedirects:false, which returns the redirect response. Conflicts with followRedirects:false. Invalid values throw code options. Copied during construction.

Ownership: borrowed at construction; settings are retained independently.

```tsrust
import { ClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const options: ClientOptions = {
    maxRedirects: 4
  };
  console.log(options.maxRedirects);
}
```

proxy.documentation:
Optional explicit proxy URL passed to the selected HTTP backend, including supported URL credentials for proxy authentication. This overrides automatic proxy discovery for this client. Conflicts with noProxy:true. Malformed proxy configuration throws HttpError code options without returning the proxy URL or credentials; transport failures throw request. No new proxy transport features are enabled. Construction borrows the string and retains independent settings.

Ownership: borrowed at construction; settings are retained independently.

```tsrust
import { ClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const options: ClientOptions = {
    proxy: "http://127.0.0.1:8080"
  };
  console.log(options.proxy);
}
```

noProxy.documentation:
Optional proxy-discovery override. True disables every proxy for this client; false, undefined or omission leaves backend discovery unchanged unless an explicit proxy is supplied. True conflicts with proxy and throws HttpError code options. This does not mutate process environment. Copied during construction.

Ownership: borrowed at construction; settings are retained independently.

```tsrust
import { ClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const options: ClientOptions = {
    noProxy: true
  };
  console.log(options.noProxy);
}
```

std:http.documentation:
Explicit listener policies passed to app.configure before listen. All seven integer fields are required when configure is called. bodyLimit and maxConnections must be positive and fit the target unsigned pointer size. maxRequests must also fit Tokio Semaphore::MAX_PERMITS. The three positive millisecond deadlines must fit the backend monotonic Instant range. requestsPerSecond is nonnegative; zero disables rate limiting. Invalid values throw HttpError code options without changing the previous configuration. An app without configure has no added body, connection, concurrency, rate or deadline policy; transport defaults still apply.

The example prints '65536'.

```tsrust
import { App, ServerOptions } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const options: ServerOptions = {
    bodyLimit: 65536, maxConnections: 64, maxRequests: 32, requestTimeoutMs: 5000, shutdownTimeoutMs: 5000, handshakeTimeoutMs: 3000, requestsPerSecond: 100
  };
  const app = new App();
  app.configure(options);
  console.log(options.bodyLimit); // 65536
}
```

Ownership: configuration borrows the options; the record remains usable.

