import { streamChannel } from 'std:http';
import { Channel, Sender } from 'std:futures';
import { Bytes, fromUtf8 } from 'std:bytes';
import { strictEqual } from 'std:testing';

async function produce(sender: Sender<Bytes>): Task<void> {
  await sender.send(unwrap(fromUtf8('hello ')));
  await sender.send(unwrap(fromUtf8('world')));
}

async function main(): Task<void> {
  const chunks = new Channel<Bytes>(1);
  const producer = produce(chunks.sender()).start();
  try {
    const response = streamChannel(chunks);
    strictEqual(await response.text(), 'hello world');
  } finally {
    await producer;
  }
}

std:http.documentation:
Creates an unsent request whose body is the byte chunks sent on `body`, without collecting them first. Sends wait when the channel is full; capacity counts chunks, not bytes.

The body ends when every sender is dropped. Dropping or cancelling the request makes pending sends fail with code `closed`. The producer task is yours to start and await. An invalid method or URL throws `HttpError` code `method` or `url`. Nothing is sent until `Client.send`, and no `Content-Type` is set.

```tsrust
const producer = produce(chunks.sender()).start();

try {
  await client.send(requestChannel('POST', url, chunks));
} finally {
  await producer;
}
```

Ownership: consumes the channel; borrows `method` and `url`.

Encodes ordered form fields with application/x-www-form-urlencoded using UTF-8, preserving duplicate names and empty values. Spaces become +; reserved bytes are percent encoded. There is no implicit field-count ceiling. The resulting buffered body has a known length and supports backend redirect replay. Invalid method or URI throws HttpError code method or url. Construction performs no network I/O. Explicit setHeader calls override factory headers.

Ownership: borrows fields during construction; the request owns the encoded body.

```tsrust
import { fromArray } from 'std:bytes';
import { requestForm, FormField } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const fields: FormField[] = [{
    name: "q", value: "é +"
  }];
  const input = requestForm("POST", "http://localhost/", fields);
  console.log(input.method(), fields.length);
}
```

Expected output: POST 1.

Creates an unsent `multipart/form-data` request from `parts`, with the boundary and `Content-Length` generated for you. Each part may set `fileName` and `contentType`; invalid MIME text throws `HttpError` code `multipart`. An invalid method or URL throws code `method` or `url`.

Use ASCII field names: the server parser does not recognize other encodings (values and file names may be Unicode). The body cannot be resent on a redirect. `setHeader` overrides the generated headers.

Ownership: consumes the parts and their buffers.

```tsrust
import { fromArray } from 'std:bytes';
import { requestMultipart, MultipartPart } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const parts: MultipartPart[] = [{
    name: "data", data: unwrap(fromArray([0, 255]))
  }];
  const input = requestMultipart("POST", "http://localhost/", parts);
  console.log(input.method());
}
```

Expected output: POST.

An owned HTTP policy installed explicitly with App.use or Router.use. A value alone changes no server. Helpers compose with authored callbacks in source order; Router path installation uses the existing route pattern rules.

Ownership: installation consumes the value. It is not implicitly cloned or globally installed.

```tsrust
import { App, Middleware, secureHeaders } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const app = new App();
  const policy: Middleware = secureHeaders();
  app.use(policy);
  console.log('installed');
}
```

Expected output: installed.

Optional CORS lists and flags. Omitted origins, methods and header lists allow nothing; credentials defaults false and maxAgeSeconds is absent. A sole * explicitly requests a wildcard, forbidden with credentials in every list. Invalid header text, method tokens, mixed wildcards or a negative maxAgeSeconds throw HttpError code options before installation. No implicit size/cardinality/time budget is added. CORS is browser access control, not authorization.

Ownership: cors borrows options during construction and retains independently validated settings.

```tsrust
import { CorsOptions } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const options: CorsOptions = {};
  console.log(options.origins === undefined);
}
```

Expected output: true.

Constructs a CORS policy without installing it. Missing or empty lists remain closed: no allow-origin/method/header permission is invented. Explicit origins compare header values; methods are valid HTTP method tokens. allowedHeaders/exposeHeaders are header names. A sole * is an explicit wildcard; credentials=true rejects a wildcard in any list. maxAgeSeconds is optional, nonnegative seconds (0 is valid); omitted leaves backend caching behavior unchanged. Invalid options throw HttpError code options before use changes a server. The backend owns normal/preflight handling and Vary.

```tsrust
app.use(cors({
  origins: ['https://app.example'], methods: ['GET']
}));
```

Ownership: borrows options only during construction; use consumes the resulting Middleware.

Constructs an explicit policy setting absent X-Content-Type-Options:nosniff, X-Frame-Options:SAMEORIGIN and Referrer-Policy:no-referrer. Deliberate application values are preserved. It also applies to error and streamed responses. It adds no deployment-dependent HSTS, CSP, COOP or COEP policy. Install with existing use in the desired source order.

Ownership: use consumes the Middleware; construction retains no caller loans.

```tsrust
import { App, secureHeaders } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const app = new App();
  app.use(secureHeaders());
  console.log('headers');
}
```

Expected output: headers.

Constructs explicit streaming response compression for gzip/x-gzip and HTTP deflate. The backend owns negotiation, default compression level, Vary and framing. It skips known bodies below 32 bytes, images, gRPC and server-sent events; existing Content-Encoding is preserved. Missing/unsupported encodings or q=0 use identity. A bare Accept-Encoding:* also uses identity in the selected backend; br/zstd are not enabled. Compression removes an obsolete Content-Length and preserves body errors and drop behavior. No whole response is buffered. Install with existing use; choose source order with other handlers.

Ownership: use consumes Middleware. Response body ownership stays exclusive and cancellation/drop releases the active source.

```tsrust
import { App, compress } from 'std:http';

function main() {
  const app = new App();
  app.use(compress());
  console.log('compression');
}
```

Expected output: compression.

Creates opt-in source-installed access logging for App.use/Router.use, including scoped Router paths and source order. Logs generated request ID, method, matched configured route template, response status and elapsed milliseconds. No body, raw URL/query, credentials or arbitrary headers are logged. Cancellation before a response has a separate request_cancelled event without a fabricated status. Context follows async work with per-poll instrumentation; no span guard crosses an await. Ownership: logger is borrowed; returned middleware owns shared backend/context handles.

```tsrust
import { Router, accessLog } from 'std:http';
import { createLogger } from 'std:log';

function main() {
  const logger = unwrap(createLogger({
    sink: 'stdout', level: 'off'
  }));
  const app = new Router();
  app.use(accessLog(logger));
  console.log('access log installed');
}
```

Expected output: access log installed.

Owned event data for a typed SSE producer channel. Required data and optional event/id/retry fields are encoded when the body polls that event. Invalid metadata fails the body after any prefix; headers cannot be rewritten. CR and LF are separate line separators; empty data alone dispatches no browser event. Ownership: the channel and encoder consume events.

```tsrust
import { SseEvent } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: SseEvent = {
    data: 'hello'
  };
  console.log(value.data);
}
```

Expected output: hello.

Optional SSE keepalive settings. With both fields absent, no keepalive is installed; text alone selects a15-second interval. Invalid text or timer ranges throw HttpError before response creation. Ownership: settings are borrowed at construction and retained independently.

```tsrust
import { SseOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: SseOptions = {
    keepAliveMs: 15000
  };
  console.log(value.keepAliveMs);
}
```

Expected output: 15000.

Optional socket buffer and message policy. Omission preserves 131072-byte read/write buffers, an unlimited write-buffer maximum, a 67108864-byte incoming-message maximum and a 16777216-byte frame maximum. Unmasked client frames remain rejected by default. Explicit null disables message/frame bounds. Invalid ranges throw HttpError code options before connect or route registration. See the individual fields for zero and cross-field constraints.

Ownership: options are borrowed during configuration; each socket owns independent buffers and settings.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketOptions = {
    readBufferSize: 4096
  };
  console.log(value.readBufferSize);
}
```

Expected output: 4096.

Optional client trust, request headers and socket policy: ca is a PEM CA bundle, headers is an ordered WebSocketHeader array, and socket is WebSocketOptions. Omission preserves verified public roots, adds no application headers and retains backend socket defaults. A custom CA replaces roots while retaining hostname/chain/validity checks. Invalid PEM or header/policy values throw HttpError; credentials are redacted from transport errors.

Ownership: connect borrows options through task settlement and retains independent transport settings; message payloads are not cloned.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketClientOptions } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketClientOptions = {};
  console.log(value.ca);
}
```

Expected output: undefined.

One client-handshake header with required string name and value fields. Names use HTTP header grammar; values may be empty but cannot contain invalid controls. Invalid headers throw HttpError code options during connect. No header is added by default, and credentials are excluded from transport error messages.

Ownership: the record is borrowed through connect settlement; parsed request metadata is retained independently.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketHeader } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketHeader = {
    name: 'origin', value: 'https://app.example'
  };
  console.log(value.name);
}
```

Expected output: origin.

Owned text-message variant with required kind: 'text' and data: string. Empty text and newlines are preserved. send consumes the message and flushes it; receive returns independent owned UTF-8 text. Configured backend message/frame limits still apply.

Ownership: text moves with the message; converting a shared receive buffer to an owned string can copy.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketText } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketText = {
    kind: 'text', data: 'hello'
  };
  console.log(value.kind);
}
```

Expected output: text.

Owned binary-message variant with required kind: 'binary' and data: Bytes. Empty bytes remain binary even when their contents are valid UTF-8. send consumes the byte owner; receive returns owned bytes subject to configured backend limits. Borrowed byte views are not accepted as owned payloads.

Ownership: outgoing storage moves; unique incoming backing storage can transfer, while shared storage can require a copy.

```tsrust
import { fromArray, length } from "std:bytes";
import { WebSocketBinary } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketBinary = {
    kind: 'binary', data: unwrap(fromArray([0, 255]))
  };
  console.log(value.kind);
}
```

Expected output: binary.

Owned close-message variant with kind: 'close', optional code: int and optional reason: string. With neither code nor reason, sends a close without status; a reason alone selects 1000. Explicit codes must be allowed 16-bit backend codes and UTF-8 reasons must fit 123 bytes. Invalid fields throw HttpError code options. Receiving close preserves backend close normalization; it is not application text or a reconnect request.

Ownership: send or close consumes the frame and its reason.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketClose } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketClose = {
    kind: 'close', code: 1000, reason: 'done'
  };
  console.log(value.kind);
}
```

Expected output: close.

Owned result of splitting a full socket, with read: WebSocketRead and write: WebSocketWrite. The reader may wait while the writer sends. Closing the writer sends close and releases that half; dropping either half leaves the transport retained by the other. Both halves must finish or drop to release the transport.

Ownership: splitting consumes the full socket; moving a field transfers its unique half, without cloning.

```tsrust
import { fromArray, toHex } from 'std:bytes';
import { Router, WebSocket, connectWebSocket } from 'std:http';

async function echo(socket: Owned<WebSocket>): Task<void> {
  const message = await socket.receive();

  if (message !== undefined) {
    await socket.send(message);
  }

  await socket.close();
}

async function main(): Task<void> {
  const app = new Router();
  app.webSocket('/echo', echo);
  const server = await app.listen('127.0.0.1:0');
  const url = 'ws://' + server.address() + '/echo';

  try {
    const socket = await connectWebSocket(url);
    const { read, write } = socket.split();
    const receiving = read.receive();
    await write.send({
      kind: 'text', data: 'hello'
    });
    const message = await receiving;

    if (message === undefined || message.kind !== 'text') {
      throw 'missing text';
    }

    console.log(message.data);
    await write.close();
    const closing = await read.receive();

    if (closing !== undefined) {
      console.log(closing.kind);
    }

    const binary = await connectWebSocket(url);
    await binary.send({
      kind: 'binary', data: unwrap(fromArray([0, 255]))
    });
    const received = await binary.receive();

    if (received === undefined || received.kind !== 'binary') {
      throw 'missing binary';
    }

    console.log(unwrap(toHex(received.data)));
    await binary.close();
  } finally {
    await server.shutdown();
  }
}
```

Expected output: hello
close
00ff.

