std:cache.documentation:
Creates an empty cache that keeps at most `capacity` entries, dropping the least recently used. `capacity` must be positive (otherwise `RangeError`); it counts entries, not bytes. Entries have no default expiry and nothing runs in the background.

Keys are one string, int, number, boolean or enum type (NaN equals NaN; `-0` is stored as `0`). Values must own their data: no views, callbacks or task handles.

```tsrust
import { LruCache } from "std:cache";

function main() {
  const cache = new LruCache<string, string>(2);
  unwrap(cache.set("a", "one"));
  console.log(cache.size());
}
```

Expected output: 1.

Ownership: the cache cannot be cloned or sent to a worker. A value read with `get` borrows the cache until its last use; use `clone(value)` or `remove` to keep it longer.

An owned result record. CacheEntry contains key/value; CacheInsertError additionally contains code and preserves rejected inputs. Generic arguments match the cache key and value types.

```tsrust
import { LruCache } from "std:cache";

function main() {
  const cache = new LruCache<string, string>(2);
  unwrap(cache.set("a", "one"));
  const replaced = unwrap(cache.set("a", "uno"));

  if (replaced !== undefined) {
    console.log(replaced.key, replaced.value);
  }
}
// prints: a one
```

Ownership: The record owns its fields. Explicit clone is available only when every field supports independent cloning. Resource fields cannot be cloned. No implicit sharing or cloning occurs.

An owned result record. CacheEntry contains key/value; CacheInsertError additionally contains code and preserves rejected inputs. Generic arguments match the cache key and value types.

```tsrust
