std:signals.documentation:
Registers Unix SIGINT/SIGTERM or Windows console interrupt/control-break immediately in an active async runtime. No arguments. Register before announcing readiness, then await receive(listener). Unix and Windows targets; other targets return unsupported. Windows events are reported as SIGINT and SIGBREAK. Registration/runtime failures return signal/runtime errors. Signal deliveries may coalesce; no counting or delivery-order guarantee.

```tsrust
const signals = unwrap(listen());
// Start service, then await receive(signals).
```

Ownership: returns a unique owned listener. Dropping releases this subscription but does not restore process-wide default signal handling.

The complete example waits with a zero-millisecond timeout, handles unsupported platforms, and prints done without requiring an external signal.

```tsrust
import { listen, receive, SignalListener, SignalError } from 'std:signals';
async function waitBriefly(listener: Mutable<SignalListener>): Task<void> {
  try {
    await receive(listener).timeout(0);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.code !== 'timeout') throw error;
  }
}

function unsupported(error: Owned<SignalError>): void {
  if (error.code !== 'unsupported') throw error;
}

async function main(): Task<void> {
  match (listen()) {
    Ok(listener) => {
      await waitBriefly(listener);
    },
    Err(error) => {
      unsupported(error);
    }
  }

  console.log('done');
}
```

Renamed imports retain this operation's contract. Call the imported operation directly; storing it as a function value is unsupported. Use an explicit async wrapper when a callable is required.

