HttpRouter.documentation:
Registers handlers for GET requests to `path`. Handlers run in registration order: matching middleware runs before `next()` and its code after `await next()` runs in reverse. Returning a `Response` stops the chain; `await next()` continues to the next match, including later routes. Literal routes do not outrank earlier parameter routes. Unmatched requests get 404. An invalid path throws `HttpError` code `route`. GET routes also answer HEAD with an empty body. Each callback receives the context and `next`.

```tsrust
app.get("/hello", async (c, next) => {
  await next();
  c.header("x-after", "yes");
}, (c) => c.text("Hello"));
```

Handlers may omit unused parameters; call `next()` at most once. Retained state must be owned and only read. Authentication and authorization remain application code.

Ownership: registration consumes the handlers' captures; each request gets its own mutable `Context`.

