}
```

Expected output: origin.

Required HTTP header value, which may be empty. Invalid control characters throw HttpError code options during connect. Credentials are not included in transport error messages. The header is borrowed and copied as request metadata; no socket payload is cloned.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketHeader } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketHeader = {
    name: 'origin', value: 'https://app.example'
  };
  console.log(value.value);
}
```

Expected output: https://app.example.

HttpWebSocketText.documentation:
Required text-message tag. It selects UTF8 data and cannot be reassigned to a different message kind. receive retains this tag and send consumes the whole message; the socket is mutated only by the awaited operation.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketText } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketText = {
    kind: 'text', data: 'hello'
  };
  console.log(value.kind);
}
```

Expected output: text.

Required valid UTF8 text, including empty strings and newlines. send consumes the message and flushes; receive returns an independent owned string. No facade size cap is added beyond configured backend limits. The backend byte-to-owned-string conversion may copy a shared receive buffer.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketText } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketText = {
    kind: 'text', data: 'hello'
  };
  console.log(value.data);
}
```

Expected output: hello.

HttpWebSocketBinary.documentation:
Required binary-message tag. It preserves binary identity even when the bytes also form valid text. send consumes the record and byte owner; receive creates an independent byte owner. Replacing this tag with text is a type error.

```tsrust
import { fromArray, length } from "std:bytes";
import { WebSocketBinary } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketBinary = {
    kind: 'binary', data: unwrap(fromArray([0, 255]))
  };
  console.log(value.kind);
}
```

Expected output: binary.

Required owned bytes, including empty payloads. Outgoing storage moves into the backend. Incoming conversion uses the maintained consuming conversion: unique backing storage can transfer, while shared backing storage requires a copy. No borrowed view is silently cloned; pass an owned Bytes value. Configured backend limits still apply.

```tsrust
import { fromArray, length } from "std:bytes";
import { WebSocketBinary } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketBinary = {
    kind: 'binary', data: unwrap(fromArray([0, 255]))
  };
  console.log(length(value.data));
}
```

Expected output: 2.

HttpWebSocketClose.documentation:
Required close-message tag. It identifies a received close frame or an explicit outgoing close. It is not ordinary application data; subsequent operations follow the backend closing state. The message is owned and consumed by send or close.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketClose } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketClose = {
    kind: 'close', code: 1000, reason: 'done'
  };
  console.log(value.kind);
}
```

Expected output: close.

Optional close status. With no code or reason the backend sends a close without a status; a supplied reason alone selects1000. Explicit codes must be allowed16-bit backend close codes. Reserved or invalid codes fail with HttpError code options. Received codes preserve backend protocol-close normalization. The scalar moves with the frame.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketClose } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketClose = {
    kind: 'close', code: 1000, reason: 'done'
  };
  console.log(value.code);
}
```

Expected output: 1000.

Optional UTF8 close reason. Omission selects no reason; with no code it selects1000. The encoded reason must fit the backend123-byte control-frame range; oversized values fail with code options. receive preserves the actual reason. Text is consumed with the frame; no reconnection is started.

```tsrust
import { WebSocketClose } from "std:http";

function main() {
  const value: WebSocketClose = {
    kind: 'close', code: 1000, reason: 'done'
  };
  console.log(value.reason);
}
```

Expected output: done.

HttpWebSocketHalves.documentation:
Owned receive half returned by split. It can hold a pending receive while the write half sends. Moving the field transfers that half; it cannot be cloned. Dropping one half leaves the transport retained by the other; both must finish or drop to release it.

```tsrust
import { fromArray, toHex } from 'std:bytes';
import { Router, WebSocket, connectWebSocket } from 'std:http';

async function echo(socket: Owned<WebSocket>): Task<void> {
  const message = await socket.receive();

  if (message !== undefined) {
    await socket.send(message);
  }

  await socket.close();
}

async function main(): Task<void> {
  const app = new Router();
  app.webSocket('/echo', echo);
  const server = await app.listen('127.0.0.1:0');
  const url = 'ws://' + server.address() + '/echo';

  try {
    const socket = await connectWebSocket(url);
    const { read, write } = socket.split();
    const receiving = read.receive();
    await write.send({
      kind: 'text', data: 'hello'
    });
    const message = await receiving;

    if (message === undefined || message.kind !== 'text') {
      throw 'missing text';
    }

    console.log(message.data);
    await write.close();
    const closing = await read.receive();

    if (closing !== undefined) {
      console.log(closing.kind);
    }

    const binary = await connectWebSocket(url);
    await binary.send({
      kind: 'binary', data: unwrap(fromArray([0, 255]))
    });
    const received = await binary.receive();

    if (received === undefined || received.kind !== 'binary') {
      throw 'missing binary';
    }

    console.log(unwrap(toHex(received.data)));
    await binary.close();
  } finally {
    await server.shutdown();
  }
}
```

Expected output: hello
close
00ff.

